\par\par \b \uc2 China Shares Take Late-Day Tumble -- 5th Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chao Deng   \par \uc2 950 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2016\par 13:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 China shares tumbled within a whisker of their August low in the last hour of trading Wednesday, a reminder of the late-afternoon swings that unsettled markets last summer.\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite Index finished down 2.4% at 2949.60, just 22 points above its 2015 low on Aug. 26, and the first close below the 3000 level since then.\par \par \uc2 The index has lost almost 17% so far this year and 43% since its June high. The swift, steep fall echoes a four-day stretch in late August, when the benchmark lost 23%.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, Asian markets rose as China's yuan steadied over the past four days, soothing worries that authorities were weakening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stimulate the country's slowing growth. Most Asian markets closed before Shanghai's slide.\par \par \uc2 Australia's S&P/ASX 200 and South Korea's Kospi both gained about 1.3%. The Nikkei Stock Average rose 2.9% as the Japanese yen slipped against the U.S. dollar. Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index, which closes after China, held on to gains, and rose 1.1%.\par \par \uc2 Investors in China are jittery as they try to figure out how much authorities will prop up the market. Analysts say some are worried officials appear to be prioritizing the yuan's stability over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lift stocks. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves recorded the biggest monthly drop ever in December, suggesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is spending huge amounts of cash to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I think whether the index is going higher or lower is no longer the focus of the National Team," says Hao Hong, managing director at Bank of Communications Co.\par \par \uc2 When shares crashed last summer, a group of state-owned funds--the "National Team"--bought heavily, often in the last hour of the day, which triggered late-day surges. Analysts estimate the National Team bought hundreds of billions of U.S. dollars.\par \par \uc2 Investors eager to lock in gains also sold heavily in the final minutes of trading. Authorities enlisted measures, including clamping down on "malicious" short sellers and freezing new public listings to steady the market, to stem selling.\par \par \uc2 Taken together, the steps largely paid off: The Shanghai Composite entered into a bull market in November, defined as a rise of 20% from a recent low. Now, the benchmark is down 19.2% from Dec. 22, close to re-entering a bear territory, defined as a drop of 20% from a recent high.\par \par \uc2 "Investors are increasingly concerned that the market cannot endure more pressure without government support," said Li Lifeng, an equity analyst at Sinolink Securities.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, blue-chips, a common target of government buying, fell 1.2%. A gauge of the sector has lost 13% this year. Some 159 stocks also fell by daily 10% downward trading limit set by authorities on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Another reminder of how the summer's tumult is still haunting shares in January is the unwinding of margin loans. Borrowing to buy stocks snowballed losses during the crash, as investors rushed to sell holdings to pay back their brokers.\par \par \uc2 Margin loans have fallen by 12% since the beginning of this year, down to 1.05 trillion yuan (US$160 billion) as of Tuesday, according to data provider Wind Information. Since early last year, some listed companies and major shareholders have pledged their own shares to obtain margin loans.\par \par \uc2 Analysts worry that liquidity could tighten if companies suspend trading. About 300 firms have suspended trading for at least one session this year, in an apparent move to avoid further losses.\par \par \uc2 Shenzhen Coship Electronics Co. suspended trading Tuesday, saying in a statement that a fall in its stock price from current levels of 10.03 yuan to between 8.5 and 9.5 yuan would trigger margin calls for loans taken out by its controlling shareholder. The shareholder has pledged most of his 16.88% stake in the firm to borrow money, according to the statement.\par \par \uc2 Another reason for Wednesday's drop could be that falling below the 3000 level triggered automatic selling, said Robert Levine, head of Asia sales trading at CLSA.\par \par \uc2 A byproduct of Shanghai's tumbling stock prices is that more Chinese companies are looking to the Hong Kong market for initial public offerings.\par \par \uc2 China BoQi Environmental Solutions Technology (Holding) Co., a Chinese power and waste-treatment company, is now planning a Hong Kong IPO after earlier considering one of China's domestic exchanges, according to a person familiar with the situation. China BoQi is now planning to relist in Hong Kong as early as the end of this year in a $300 million deal, according to people with knowledge of the matter.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed the yuan at 6.5630 to one U.S. dollar on Wednesday, marking the fourth day that it has kept its yuan guidance roughly stable. The onshore yuan can trade up or down within 2% of the bank's daily guidance.\par \par \uc2 The onshore yuan was last at 6.5762, roughly unchanged from 6.5756 late Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen was last down 0.5% at Yen117.80 to one U.S. dollar, lifting the Nikkei, as a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps Japanese exporters that repatriate overseas earnings.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, U.S. oil dipped below $30 a barrel. Brent crude oil, the international benchmark, recovered in Asia, last up 0.7% at $31.17 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Gold prices were flat at $1,084.70 a troy ounce.\par \par \uc2 Yifan Xie and Kane Wu contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Chao Deng at Chao.Deng@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 13, 2016 07:30 ET (12:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160113ec1d001fq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
