\par\par \b \uc2 Stocks Push Higher in Final Hour of Trading\b0\par\par\uc2 By Saumya Vaishampayan and Corrie Driebusch   \par \uc2 820 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2016\par 21:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks pushed higher heading into the close in a volatile session Tuesday, while U.S. oil prices declined for the seventh straight session.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average gained 86 points, or 0.5%, to 16485, after declining as much as 77 points. The S&P 500 rose 0.7%, while the Nasdaq Composite was 0.9% higher.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500's index of energy companies gained 0.2%, even as oil prices briefly dropped below $30 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 While lower oil prices are traditionally viewed as a boon for consumers and the economy more broadly, recent moves downward have raised the specter of bankruptcies in the energy sector, weighing on broader indexes.\par \par \uc2 Williams Cos\uc2 . shares tumbled 11% to $16.58. Mining company \uc2 Freeport-McMoRan Inc\uc2 . fell 5.8% to $4.06. The company's shares are down close to 40% so far this year.\par \par \uc2 The moves came as Chinese stocks managed to eke out a gain. Sharp declines in Chinese shares have rattled global markets this year.\par \par \uc2 "China had a considerably less volatile session than we had the last several weeks so that helped calm sentiment a bit, but this decline that started out of China continues to persist," said Ryan Larson, head of U.S. equity trading for \uc2 RBC Global Asset Management\uc2 . "China is still slowing, and oil is now flirting with $30."\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite inched up 0.2% after swinging between gains and losses during the session.\par \par \uc2 Investors also pointed to moves in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The onshore Chinese yuan steadied for the third day in a row after weakening sharply last week and the offshore yuan rose to its highest level against the U.S. dollar so far this year.\par \par \uc2 The offshore yuan rallied as state-owned banks bought the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sold U.S. dollars aggressively, traders said, a sign that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to narrow the gap between the onshore and offshore rates, which closed briefly Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 As a result, liquidity tightened in the offshore market, sending the benchmark overnight borrowing costs for the offshore yuan to a high. Higher borrowing costs make it more expensive to bet against the yuan--traders have to borrow yuan to sell, or short, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --thus putting a floor under the offshore yuan market.\par \par \uc2 A stronger dollar and failure to stem a global glut of supply have weighed on oil prices in recent months, while mounting concerns about China's slowing growth have magnified fears about demand from the world's second-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 Traditional haven assets lost a bit of ground Tuesday. Gold fell 0.6% to $1,090 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar was up 0.3% against the yen at Yen117.9530, while the euro was down 0.2% against the dollar at $1.0837.\par \par \uc2 The British pound fell 0.8% against the dollar to $1.4434, its lowest level since 2010, as unexpectedly weak U.K. industrial data added to concerns about the manufacturing sector. The \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  will hold its first policy meeting of the year this week, and economists forecast no change to benchmark interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's moves came after the Dow industrials snapped a losing streak on Monday in a rocky session.\par \par \uc2 After U.S. markets closed, aluminum maker \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  unofficially kicked off the fourth-quarter earnings season as it swung to a loss. Shares are down 9.2% at $7.26.\par \par \uc2 Corporate profits are expected to take a hit from the stronger dollar, slowing Chinese growth and sliding oil prices.\par \par \uc2 Investors are eyeing fourth-quarter earnings in hopes that better-than-expected corporate results may help buoy stocks. However, some remain wary of whether earnings will deliver.\par \par \uc2 "Valuations have been on the higher side...so it leaves us in a bit of a precarious position," said Eric Wiegand, senior portfolio manager at U.S. Bank Wealth Management. "If we don't see the earnings growth to support that, we could see again an environment of enhanced volatility," he added.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 recently traded at 17.4 times the past 12 months of earnings, higher than its 10-year average of 15.7, according to FactSet.\par \par \uc2 The moves in U.S. stocks come at an uncertain time for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , which is watching economic data closely as it considers the path for raising interest rates. The U.S. economy likely will withstand financial market volatility if it doesn't last too long, allowing more interest-rate increases this year, Federal Reserve Bank of Atlanta President \uc2 Dennis Lockhart\uc2  said Monday.\par \par \uc2 Corrie Driebusch and \uc2 Riva Gold\uc2  contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Leslie Josephs at leslie.josephs@wsj.com and Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 12, 2016 15:59 ET (20:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160112ec1c0039d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 European Markets Snapped Their Recent Losing Streak\b0\par\par\uc2 671 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2016\par \uc2 RTT News\par \uc2 RTTNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2016 NoticiasFinancieras. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (RTTNews) - The European markets have been under pressure for the last few sessions, but ended Tuesday's session solidly in positive territory. However, the markets finished off their session highs, as they pared early gains going into the close.\par \par \uc2 Retailers were among the best performing stocks Tuesday, after positive sales data from Metro and \uc2 Wm Morrison Supermarkets\uc2 . Auto makers also turned in a strong performance after \uc2 Peugeot\uc2  reported sales results.\par \par \uc2 Most Asian markets dropped Tuesday, but China's stock market finished with a modest gain. China's offshore yuan firmed above the onshore rate for the first time since October, spurred by what traders called aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Beijing.\par \par \uc2 Ma Jun, the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's chief economist, said that \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to keep the yuan basically stable against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and fluctuations of the yuan against the dollar will increase.\par \par \uc2 The Euro Stoxx 50 index of eurozone bluechip stocks increased 1.23 percent, while the Stoxx Europe 50 index, which includes some major U.K. companies, added 0.97 percent.\par \par \uc2 The DAX of Germany climbed 1.64 percent and the CAC 40 of France rose 1.53 percent. The FTSE of the U.K. gained 0.98 percent and the SMI of Switzerland finished higher by 1.44 percent.\par \par \uc2 In Frankfurt, \uc2 SAP\uc2  increased 3.66 percent after it reported preliminary results for the fourth quarter and full year 2015.\par \par \uc2 Metro gained 3.62 percent. The company reported that preliminary like-for-like sales for the first-quarter of 2015/16 rose 0.1%, compared to an increase of 2.3% last year.\par \par \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  rose 3.59 percent and Daimler advanced 1.83 percent. \uc2 BMW\uc2  finished higher by 2.15 percent and \uc2 Continental\uc2  added 2.06 percent.\par \par \uc2 In Paris, \uc2 Peugeot\uc2  climbed 4.76 percent. The company said it sold 2.973 million vehicles worldwide in 2015, up 1.2% from last year.\par \par \uc2 Renault increased 2.90 percent and \uc2 Valeo\uc2  added 2.68 percent.\par \par \uc2 In London, \uc2 Wm Morrison Supermarkets\uc2  jumped 8.67 percent. In the nine weeks to January 3, the retailer's like-for-like sales, excluding fuel, were up 0.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 Tesco\uc2  surged 6.70 percent and \uc2 J Sainsbury\uc2  gained 3.25 percent. \uc2 Burberry\uc2  also finished higher by percent.\par \par \uc2 Department store group \uc2 Debenhams\uc2  said in its 19-week trading update, that group gross transaction value increased 2.5 percent, and group like-for-like sales went up 1.9 percent on a reported basis and 3.5 percent in constant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The stock surged 15.91 percent on the news.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, staffing firm \uc2 Michael Page\uc2  declined 8.08 percent. The company experienced tougher trading conditions in Asia Pacific and the U.K. in the fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 Gemalto\uc2  climbed 8.76 percent in Amsterdam. Quebec's driver's license and vehicle registration authority, SAAQ, has declared \uc2 Gemalto\uc2 's new central issuance facility for driver's licenses \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The French economy is forecast to expand 0.3 percent in the fourth quarter, unrevised from the previous estimate, \uc2 the Bank of France\uc2  said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The business confidence index rose slightly to 99 in December, while it was forecast to remain unchanged at 98.\par \par \uc2 Industrial production increased in December. According to business managers, production will increase again in January.\par \par \uc2 U.K. industrial production declined at the fastest pace in almost three years in November as warm weather reduced energy demand. Industrial output slid unexpectedly by 0.7 percent month-on-month after staying flat in October, figures from the \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  showed Tuesday. Production was forecast to grow 0.1 percent in November.\par \par \uc2 Like-for-like sales in the United Kingdom were up just 0.1 percent on year in December, the \uc2 British Retail Consortium\uc2  said on Tuesday. That was well shy of forecasts for an increase of 0.5 percent following the 0.4 percent contraction in November.\par \par \uc2 For comments and feedback: contact editorial@rttnews.com\par \par \uc2 Copyright(c) 2016 RTTNews.com. All Rights Reserved\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTTNEW0020160112ec1c005eh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 China Takes More Steps To Stabilize Yuan\b0\par\par\uc2 277 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2016\par \uc2 RTT News\par \uc2 RTTNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2016 NoticiasFinancieras. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (RTTNews) - Chinese authorities adopted more measures to boost investor confidence that saw markets close higher on Tuesday, though they remained highly volatile.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  reportedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market aggressively that led the rates of the onshore yuan and the Hong Kong-based offshore yuan, known as the CNH, hit parity.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently instructed the state-owned banks to buy yuan offshore massively. The spread between the onshore and offshore yuan rates was more than 2 percent last week that fueled arbitrage between the two rates.\par \par \uc2 The PBoC actions saw the overnight yuan borrowing rates in Hong Kong, which is the Hong Kong Interbank Offering Rate (HIBOR), surge a record 66.80 percent on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, Chinese government officials were also trying to boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The PBoC Chief Economist Ma Jun said Tuesday that the bank aims to stabilize the yuan and to tie the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He also noted that the yuan-dollar volatility will continue.\par \par \uc2 Han Jun\uc2 , deputy director of the office of the Central Leading Group on Financial and Economic Affairs, said on Monday that it was "ridiculous" to bet on a large depreciation in the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Authorities are trying to fight speculation that the yuan is a one-way depreciation bet, analysts said. They also want to make the yuan exchange rate more market-determined ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inclusion into the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's reserve-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or SDR basket later this year.\par \par \uc2 For comments and feedback: contact editorial@rttnews.com\par \par \uc2 Copyright(c) 2016 RTTNews.com. All Rights Reserved\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTTNEW0020160112ec1c004xt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
