\par\par \b \uc2 Stocks Don't Go Straight Down, Either -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2016\par 13:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 7:30 ET - US stock-index futures are maintaining their decent gains as major European bourses are up 1.5-2.0% amid a partial rebound from equities' start-of-year slump. Obviously stocks are oversold on a short-term basis considering the size of the past 2 weeks' decline, so a bounce of at least 1 day is inevitable. What remains to be seen is if this selloff is like so many others of the past several years--one which gets reversed at least as quickly as prices fell. That didn't happen in August; will the same occur now? S&P 500 futures are up 15 as \uc2 Nymex\uc2  oil and gold are down modestly amid slight gains for the dollar; the euro is at $1.0855. Treasurys are also down, putting the 10-year yield at 2.19%. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 7:04 ET - \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  (PBR) joins the parade of lowered oil-price views, cutting its projected 2016 average for Brent to $45/barrel from $55. This as it now sees the dollar averaging BRL4.06, not BRL3.8 as before. The updates are part of the Brazilian oil company's revised 2015-9 business plan, which includes a 24% cut in previously projected capital spending. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1016 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Security will set the foundation for U.S. engagement in Southeast Asia during President Barack Obama's final year in office as Washington seeks to build on common ground in the region, James R. Keith, a senior director at U.S.-based international advisory firm McLarty Associates, said during a visit to Jakarta Tuesday. "If you can have a platform that you can manage on the security side, then you can talk about the economic agenda and how to advance American interests," he said. Obama will join several Asia-focused summits in 2016, starting next month when he meets with the 10 leaders of the \uc2 Association of Southeast Asian Nations\uc2  in Sunnylands, California. Nelson Cunningham, head of McLarty Associates, said trans-Pacific trade, economic development and security in the South China Sea are likely to be prominent topics during that gathering.(sara.schonhardt@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0953 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Thailand's exports to Europe look unlikely to emerge from contraction in 2016 due to a series of negative factors, according to Kasikorn Research Center. Official data show Thai exports to \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  during the first 11 months of 2015 fell 6.4% on-year to $18.13 billion. Kasikorn expects Thai shipments to \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  to contract between 1.0% and 3.0% this year, easing from the 7% contraction estimate for 2015, while noting that factors which will continue putting pressure on the country's shipments include the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2 's end of tax privileges for Thai merchandises and the bloc's complaints about illegal, unreported and unregulated activities in the country's fishing industry which may lead to an import ban.(nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0943 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Hang Seng Index reversed morning gains to plunge 0.9% to 19,712 Tuesday, dragged down by losses in large-cap telecom and oil stocks. The market was also shaken by China's latest moves to unwind foreign investors' bets against the offshore yuan, after Hong Kong-based Chinese banks bought up yuan acting on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , squeezing liquidity and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  short-sellers. "It's this 'carry trade' of people speculating on a further depreciation of the RMB that the PBoC looks to be trying to shut the door on," says a Parry International note. Analysts say crude-oil prices have room to fall further, which should continue to weigh heavily on Chinese oil firms. A fresh batch of China economic data due this week, including possibly softer trade figures and money supply and credit growth in December, may also add to investors' unease. (dominique.fong@wsj.com; @dominiquefong) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0925 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Analysts say the impact of the interbank rate increase on Hong Kong lenders is likely to be muted. "It's more of a story of speculators, people betting on the depreciation of the renminbi. It's less about liquidity profiles of banks," said Sonny Hsu, senior analyst at Moody's Investors Service. For most banks, yuan-denominated lending is a small part of their balance sheet and banks generally rely on yuan deposits to fund loans, said Mr. Hsu. That means lenders rely less on the interbank market for funding, which should generally insulate them from a rate increase. (julie.steinberg@wsj.com; @jbsteins) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KBC \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0920 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] "Sucker punched by China" is how one multi-billion dollar hedge fund manager feels about the start to the year. It isn't just the market mayhem, but also the sudden reversals in Beijing's plans. Who would have thought by the second week in January, at least four Chinese financial market policies would have been tossed out like cheap trash. Stock market circuit breakers were rightfully suspended days after they were introduced. The lifting of temporary restrictions on major shareholders selling was for all intents and purposes nixed. The daily yuan fixing, which was meant to follow market forces, has instead followed the hand of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . And now China's great experiment in internationalization of the yuan has been squelched by an effective clampdown in offshore yuan trading in Hong Kong. How should investors prepare for the rest of the year? Watch out for more punches. (alex.frangos@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0918 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Hong Kong banks do tap the interbank market for funds. But many generally rely on yuan deposits to fund their loans, said Michael Wu, senior equity analyst based in Hong Kong at Morningstar Investment Management Asia. For banks that don't have enough deposits and need to borrow, a higher interbank rate could in turn crimp their lending. Still, the effect on Hong Kong banks' overall \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to be muted, as banks have been paring back their yuan-lending businesses amid China's economic slowdown. "Renminbi lending isn't big in the context of their overall loan book," Mr. Wu said. "Therefore the impact wouldn't be huge." (julie.steinberg@wsj.com; @jbsteins) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KBC \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0916 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] JF Apex Research expects Malaysia's industrial production index for December to rise by 2.5% year-on-year on the back of projected expansion in exports. The research house notes the slowdown in China but maintains that higher demand for Malaysian electricals and electronics products will continue to support local manufacturing and industrial production. The comments come after Malaysia's November industrial production missed expectations and reported an on-year growth of 1.8%. This was a slower from 4.2% growth in the index in October. Exports for November also missed expectations and grew by 6.3% year-on-year. JF Apex Research expects the rise in exports to continue and prop up local manufacturing. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0908 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Liquidity squeeze in the offshore yuan, or CNH, market pushed the benchmark overnight interbank borrowing rate in Hong Kong, or Hibor, to 66.815%. Traders say the market rate was once close to 110%. These are extreme levels and the scarce liquidity is unprecedented in the CNH market. To senior traders, though, who've been here for decades, it's not new. In the Asian Financial Crisis in 1997, fears that the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar might break forced the local banks to lift interest rates sharply in defense of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The overnight Hibor for the Hong Kong dollar soared to an intraday high of 300%, effectively raising costs for speculators to borrow and short the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Traders, though, expect the CNH liquidity squeeze this time won't have a huge impact on the onshore money market rates and the real economy in Hong Kong and China, whereas 19 years ago, the squeezed up Hibor rate triggered tremendous selloff in Hong Kong stocks. (fiona.law@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0905 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  declared war on offshore \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  speculators this week by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily in the CNH market. But this doesn't address the root causes of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  conundrum. Dominating the Hong Kong market is easy, but stopping outflows from mainland \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expensive. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spent $130 billion on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in December alone, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  may hope that bringing the CNH into line with the CNY will lessen depreciation expectations, but there are fundamental forces pushing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  down. Growth is slowing and interest rates are falling in China, driving down investment returns available there, even as the U.S. is set to keep raising rates. Despite the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's tactical victories this week, the outlook for the yuan isn't positive. (aaron.back@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: \f28 \uc2 \f2 ACM\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0902 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The effects of the offshore yuan's benchmark overnight Hong Kong interbank offered rate increase could be psychological for investors, says one banker in Hong Kong. "The question mark now is how long they can keep [the rate high]," said a banker in Hong Kong. Keeping it high "for a long period of time takes confidence away from the CNH market," he said, which may end up being seen as "illiquid and untradeable." There is a "psychological impact," he added, and the potential for investors to avoid a market "they were being encouraged to trade in." (julie.steinberg@wsj.com; @jbsteins) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KBC \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 12, 2016 07:30 ET (12:30 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160112ec1c001e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
