\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervening \highlight\uc2  to support yuan via state-owned banks - traders\b0\par\par\uc2 163 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2016\par 03:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Jan 8 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trading to support the yuan via state-owned banks, traders said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "State-owned banks were offering dollar liquidity around 6.59, suspected to be on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a trader at a European bank in Shanghai. "This happened both today and yesterday."\par \par \uc2 Early on Friday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened its official rate for the first time in nine trading days.\par \par \uc2 China allowed the biggest fall in the yuan in five months on Thursday, pressuring regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sending global markets tumbling as investors feared it would trigger competitive devaluations.\par \par \uc2 In spot trade, yuan opened at 6.5700 and was changing hands at 6.5888 by 0225 GMT, compared with its previous close of 6.5929.\par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Lu Jianxin and Pete Sweeney; Editing by Kim Coghill)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-11T02:27:03.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160108ec18002mi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
