\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Strong U.S. jobs data support dollar, China worries persist\b0\par\par\uc2 537 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2016\par 16:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Dollar gains briefly on Dec payrolls report\par \par \uc2 * Little U.S. wage growth raises doubt on more rate hikes\par \par \uc2 * Investors cautious on long dollar positions due to China\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 PBOC\uc2  sets higher yuan fix for first time in 9 days (Updates market action, changes dateline, previous LONDON)\par \par \uc2 By Richard Leong\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 8 (Reuters) - The dollar climbed Friday on measures taken by China to ease this week's market turmoil and a hefty rise in U.S. jobs in December, but gains were limited by worries over whether Beijing has done enough to calm its battered stock market.\par \par \uc2 This week's steep losses across global stock markets due to fears about further slowing in the world's second-biggest economy have clouded investors' outlook on the greenback and whether the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has room to raise U.S. interest rates further, if at all, in 2016, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "The market's reaction is something between curious and concerning," Richard Scalone, co-head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at TJM Brokerage in Chicago said of the December U.S. payrolls report.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's rebound from a drop Thursday picked up after data that showed U.S. employers added 292,000 workers in December, well exceeding the 200,000 forecast of economists polled by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Those gains then faded as traders focused on the absence of wage growth last month, which analysts reckoned caused U.S. inflation to struggle to rise to 2 percent, the Fed's goal.\par \par \uc2 "This suggests inflation may be weak in 2016. It would be hard for them to deliver four hikes this year," said Charles St-Arnaud, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Nomura Securities\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 U.S. interest rates futures implied traders have priced in two U.S. rate increases in 2016, half the increases hinted by Fed Vice Chair \uc2 Stanley Fischer\uc2  earlier this week, according to \uc2 CME Group\uc2 's FedWatch program.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index, which measures the greenback against a basket of six \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was up 0.3 percent at 98.546 which was far below its session high of 99.183. It posted its biggest one-day decline in about a month on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The euro was down 0.3 percent against the dollar at $1.0895 , while the greenback clung to a 0.2 percent gain at 117.87 yen, pulling away from a 4-1/2-month low of 117.33 on Thursday, according to EBS data.\par \par \uc2 While traders sought to figure out the implication of the latest U.S. jobs data on the Fed's rate-hike plan, they remain jittery about China, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 The yuan, down by up to 3 percent in offshore trading this week, steadied, with dealers reporting both outright \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-owned banks and temporary bans on Chinese banks selling dollars.\par \par \uc2 The yuan was fixed higher by the PBOC for the first time in nine days on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Sources told Reuters that \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is under increasing pressure from policy advisers to let the yuan fall potentially by another 10-15 percent. (Additional reporting by Patrick Graham in London; Editing by Bernadette Baum) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-8T16:40:59.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160108ec1800kgb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
