\par\par \b \uc2 Australian Dollar Rebounds From Three-Month Low\b0\par\par\uc2 By Vera Sprothen   \par \uc2 319 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2016\par 08:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY--The Australian dollar rebounded from a three-month low Friday, as China managed to arrest a multi-day selloff in global financial markets by refraining from further weakening the yuan.\par \par \uc2 At 0530 GMT, the Australian dollar was trading at US$0.7054, about 0.7% higher than during late New York trading Thursday, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slumped to US$0.7005, the lowest level since Oct. 2.\par \par \uc2 Other commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the Canadian dollar and Norwegian krone, also recovered on the back of a mild turnaround in global oil prices. Stocks in Australia stabilized, while Chinese equities rose following days of heavy falls that triggered the Shanghai market to shut down twice this week.\par \par \uc2 Investors, highly nervous after a series of confusing market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Chinese authorities, appeared to regain some confidence when \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  guided the yuan stronger Friday in its daily exchange-rate fixing.\par \par \uc2 Still, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and equity markets will likely continue to be jittery in coming days amid signs that global growth remains fragile and constrained, cautions Shane Oliver, chief economist at AMP Capital in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 "The poor start to the year clearly warns that global growth concerns remain, that commodity prices are still under downward pressure and that volatility in investment markets will likely remain high," said Mr. Oliver.\par \par \uc2 He expects the Australian dollar to continue its fall to around US$0.60 by year-end, as commodity prices remain weak and U.S. interest rates are set to increase further this year, dampening the yield appeal of Australian government bonds over U.S. Treasurys.\par \par \uc2 Fresh domestic economic data failed to move the Australian dollar Friday. Retail sales rose in November, but were slightly lower than expected, the \uc2 Australian Bureau of Statistics\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Vera Sprothen at vera.sprothen@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 08-01-16 0705GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020160108ec1800059\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
