\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED-UPDATE 1-Japan's Aso: China may find it difficult to continue buying yuan\b0\par\par\uc2 318 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2016\par 01:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Corrects second paragraph to show Aso did not comment on capital flight or competitive devaluation)               \par \par \uc2 * Yuan declines raise fears of capital flight               \par \par \uc2 * China's policymakers struggle with slowing economy               \par \par \uc2 * Further yuan declines could roil global economy               \par \par \uc2 By Stanley White               \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 8 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Taro Aso said on Friday that China may find it difficult to continue supporting the yuan given the record decline in its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves - a warning that China may have to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall even more.               \par \par \uc2 Further declines could send shockwaves around the world as they could trigger further capital flight from China and potentially set off competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations in other countries, some economists have said.               \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have already fallen this much due to China's purchases of yuan to support its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so it could be difficult to continue," Aso said.               \par \par \uc2 China's stock markets have also been in free fall since the start of the year, raising concern Chinese authorities are losing grip of the decelerating economy.               \par \par \uc2 China has recently accelerated the depreciation of the yuan, which some economists take as an indication that Beijing realises it cannot continue to burn through its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.               \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's largest, posted their biggest annual drop on record in 2015, data showed on Thursday.               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell $512.66 billion in 2015 to $3.33 trillion, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed.               \par \par \uc2 They dropped $107.9 billion in December alone, the biggest monthly decline on record and more than markets had expected. Economists polled by Reuters had expected reserves to end the year at $3.40 trillion.               \par \par \uc2 Aso also expressed concern about the disparity between the onshore and offshore rates of the yuan caused by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-8T01:56:58.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160108ec18000p3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
