\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-Pressure on \uc2 China  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  for bigger yuan depreciation - sources\b0\par\par\uc2 916 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2016\par 00:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story that first moved late Thursday)\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 PBOC\uc2  policy of gradual depreciation under fire - insiders\par \par \uc2 * Yuan slide on Thursday blamed for stock market drop\par \par \uc2 * Reflects rising concern about weak economic fundamentals\par \par \uc2 * Some want a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cushion industry restructuring\par \par \uc2 * Risks include trade tensions, damaged confidence in yuan\par \par \uc2 By Kevin Yao and Pete Sweeney\par \par \uc2 BEIJING/SHANGHAI, Jan 8 (Reuters) - \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is under increasing pressure from policy advisers to let the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall quickly and sharply, by as much as 10-15 percent, as its recent gradual softening is thought to be doing more harm than good.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 has spent billions of dollars buying yuan over recent months to defend the exchange rate, but has failed to stabilise market sentiment. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has steadily lost another 2.6 percent against the U.S. dollar even after the bank sprung a surprise devaluation of nearly 2 percent in August.\par \par \uc2 That gradual, managed depreciation makes the yuan a one-way bet for investors who see the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop it up.\par \par \uc2 Policy insiders are now calling for a quick and sharp yuan depreciation, backed by tighter capital controls to curb speculation and the flight of money out of the country.\par \par \uc2 "We should let the yuan have a considerable depreciation, but we should have a bottom line; it cannot create a big impact on the economy and the financial system, and big panic in the capital market," an influential government economist told Reuters, suggesting the yuan be allowed to depreciate by 10-15 percent over an unspecified timeframe.\par \par \uc2 Letting the yuan fall sharply and quickly could help cushion many of China's debt-laden companies as the government pursues far-reaching structural reforms. Beijing is keen to restructure industry through "supply side" reform, especially reducing industrial over-capacity, but fears the corporate sector is too weak to handle that.\par \par \uc2 INDUSTRY BURDEN\par \par \uc2 To restructure without triggering mass bankruptcies and redundancies, sources said the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is being encouraged to let the yuan fall, keeping downward pressure on interest rates and relieving some of the debt servicing burden on businesses.\par \par \uc2 "If the economy slows sharply and we can't stabilize employment, how can you push reforms?" asked the influential government economist.\par \par \uc2 "The yuan should depreciate at least 10 percent to have any impact on exports ... but I don't think the authorities will take this step," said a researcher at the commerce ministry. "If China wants to rely on expanding exports to spur growth, other countries may follow suit."\par \par \uc2 While a weaker yuan would make Chinese exports cheaper overseas, and foreign products more expensive in China, it would be unlikely to go down well among the country's trading partners. The United States, for one, regularly accuses Beijing of manipulating its exchange rate to dump under-priced goods on foreign markets.\par \par \uc2 HIGH COST\par \par \uc2 The cost of the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been high. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell by more than half a trillion dollars last year as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought yuan to support the exchange rate, with reserves dropping by a record $107.9 billion in December alone.\par \par \uc2 And the yuan's continued slide since August is impacting other Chinese financial markets.\par \par \uc2 When the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  weakened its morning yuan guidance rate by 0.5 percent on Thursday, stock market investors panicked and pushed the CSI300 index down 7 percent within the first half hour, triggering circuit breakers and bringing the day's trading to a premature halt - for the second time in only the first week that these mechanisms have been in \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Policy advisers are worried the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's gradualist approach risks reinforcing expectations for more depreciation - a sort of self-fulfilling prophecy - and a thesis supported by a sharper fall in the offshore exchange rate, which is not regulated by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , than in the onshore rate.\par \par \uc2 "Because of depreciation expectations, residents and firms want to convert their yuan into dollars. Those expectations are man-made," said another policy adviser, reflecting a common view. "Gradual depreciation sends a signal to the market (that) the yuan still has room to depreciate further. This helps form the one-sided expectations."\par \par \uc2 Sources cautioned that the debate over whether and how to depreciate had not yet been settled, especially as depreciation has knock-on risks on other projects, such as increasing the international use of the yuan for trade and investment - which stronger capital controls would reverse.\par \par \uc2 And there's no guarantee it would work. The August move, which was also supposed to serve as a sort of floor for the market, ended up having the opposite effect.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has so far publicly resisted calls for sharp yuan falls. PBOC Vice Governor \uc2 Yi Gang\uc2  said on Dec. 1 there was no basis for continued depreciation, and an editorial on the bank's website on Thursday reiterated that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  can keep the yuan "basically stable at a reasonable equilibrium level" despite "speculating forces."\par \par \uc2 "The biggest risk in China is not really the economy," said Qian Wang, senior Asia economist for Vanguard Investments Hong Kong. "The real risk is, number one; the policy uncertainty, and number two; the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . China is walking on eggshells."\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Kevin Yao and Pete Sweeney, with additional reporting by Lu Jianxin and Elzio Barreto; Editing by Ian Geoghegan) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-8T00:57:42.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160107ec1700wsl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
