\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 8-China lets yuan slide, starts fight to halt turbulence\b0\par\par\uc2 1,000 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2016\par 17:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets yuan at lowest since March 2011\par \par \uc2 * Dealers also cite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as offshore rates bounce\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  stock markets tumble 7.3 pct, trigger trading halt\par \par \uc2 * Stock exchanges abandon new circuit breaker for markets\par \par \uc2 By Lu Jianxin and Patrick Graham\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI/LONDON, Jan 7 (Reuters) - China allowed the biggest fall in the yuan in five months on Thursday, pressuring regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sending global stock markets tumbling as investors feared it would trigger competitive devaluations.\par \par \uc2 For the second time this week China's stock markets were suspended for the day before an announcement late in the evening in Shanghai that authorities were abandoning the new circuit-breaking mechanism for halting trade in overly volatile markets.\par \par \uc2 That heightened anticipation about how Chinese markets may respond on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  shocked traders by setting the official midpoint rate on the yuan, also known as the renminbi (RMB), 0.5 percent weaker at 6.5646 per dollar on Thursday, the lowest since March 2011.\par \par \uc2 That tracked record losses in the more open offshore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and was the biggest daily fall since an abrupt devaluation of nearly 2 percent last August.\par \par \uc2 But dealers said the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  had then \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to reverse a more than 1 percent fall in offshore rates for the yuan after they hit a record low of 6.7600 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The yuan took back all of its losses to stand a quarter of a percent stronger at 6.6755 in European and U.S. trade.\par \par \uc2 "It's very similar to the previous round (in August) where they weaken the official rate and then \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar offshore to beat back the speculators," said a yuan trader with one international bank in London.\par \par \uc2 "That would be a way of starting to stabilise the market."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System (CFETS) repeated on Thursday that there was no basis for the yuan's continuous depreciation and that it was stable against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2015.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fixings have also helped drive the yuan down this week against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including a 3.5 percent fall against the yen and 0.8 percent against the euro.\par \par \uc2 That raised concerns that China might be aiming for a competitive devaluation to help its struggling exporters.\par \par \uc2 "That's the fear of the market," said Sim Moh Siong, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for \uc2 Bank of Singapore\uc2 , adding that it was a zero sum game as other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened in response, and the end result would be greater volatility.\par \par \uc2 Others were unsure what policy Beijing was pursuing.\par \par \uc2 "Frankly speaking, we are still not quite sure where the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  boundary is at the current stage," said Singapore-based \uc2 Oversea-Chinese Banking Corporation\uc2  (OCBC).\par \par \uc2 "The fear of the unknown has become the largest risk for RMB in the near term, despite China's sizable current account surplus."\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar <AUD-D4>, often used by \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers as a liquid proxy for the yuan, fell more than half a U.S. cent. The Korean won, however, recovered almost all of its initial falls with banks saying \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  had probably also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 OCBC noted that against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the RMB index was still only fractionally down for 2016.\par \par \uc2 ANZ bank said in a note that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's action would nevertheless "create one-way expectation of RMB depreciation, propelling capital flight and leading to significant financial instability".\par \par \uc2 DECLINING RESERVES\par \par \uc2 Data on Thursday showed China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell by the most on record last month, down $108 billion in December alone and by $513 billion overall last year.\par \par \uc2 That suggests an accelerating outflow of money from China which may largely be the result of the opening up of its financial markets over the past year, but also a sign that the world's second-largest economy is in deepening trouble.\par \par \uc2 Michael Every, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2 's Head of Markets Research, Asia-Pacific, said once Beijing had won the diplomatic triumph of getting the yuan included in the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket in November, he expected policymakers would let it slip to cope with a slowing, deflationary economy.\par \par \uc2 "Why people are panicked is because (i) they didn't see this coming, and/or (ii) the global economy needs a consumer of last resort, and China is sending a signal that they won't be it," he added.\par \par \uc2 A sustained depreciation in the yuan puts pressure on other Asian countries to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and makes commodities denominated in U.S. dollars more expensive for Chinese buyers, which could further depress demand and commodity prices.\par \par \uc2 Shanghai stocks slid 7.3 percent to trigger the halt in trading, a repeat performance of Monday's sudden tumble. Japan's Nikkei shed 2.3 percent in sympathy, and Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index was down 2.8 percent.\par \par \uc2 The halt mechanism, intended to calm market volatility, was instead "killing investors" and creating panic, a retail investor in Guangzhou complained.\par \par \uc2 China's securities regulator also unveiled new rules on Thursday to restrict selling by big shareholders who have been locked into their holdings for six months since Beijing banned them from offloading stocks to arrest a summer market crash.\par \par \uc2 In rules that take effect on Jan. 9, they can't sell more than 1 percent of a listed company's share capital every three months.\par \par \uc2 "This is crazy," said Alberto Forchielli, founder of \uc2 Mandarin Capital Partners\uc2 . "Chinese regulators set off on this path in July and they cannot get out of it. They have ruined whatever hope investors still had in the market."\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Lu Jianxin, Lee Chyen Yee and Samuel Shen in Shanghai and Patrick Graham in London; Additional reporting by Lisa Jucca and Masayuki Kitano; Writing by Wayne Cole and Will Waterman; Editing by Ruth Pitchford) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-7T17:57:41.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160107ec17002ha\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
