\par\par \b \uc2 China's Shortest Day Will Prolong the Pain -- Heard on the Street\b0\par\par\uc2 By Aaron Back   \par \uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2016\par 14:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By dragging out the pain of its stock- and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market adjustments, China is only ensuring that things will get worse before they get better.\par \par \uc2 Chinese stocks had just about the shortest trading session possible Thursday morning, thanks to a tempest set off by China's own depreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Within half an hour of opening, the CSI 300, a blue-chip index, was down 7%, triggering "circuit breakers" that halted trading for the day.\par \par \uc2 This system has been in place for less than a week, but it isn't too early to judge it a failure. The circuit breaker incentivizes traders to unload shares immediately once a selloff starts, to beat the market shutdown. That trading was halted completely by 10 a.m. also made it impossible for equities to recover any ground, as they well might have once the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The fundamental problem is that Chinese stocks and its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are both overvalued, and Beijing is afraid to let them adjust. Shanghai-traded shares are still above their historical valuations, and at a big premium to virtually identical shares in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 During last summer's meltdown, China's securities regulator banned shareholders who own 5% or more of a company from selling any of their holdings for six months. It doesn't take a market genius to recognize that as Friday's scheduled expiration of the ban approached, traders might get nervous.\par \par \uc2 So on Thursday, the regulator doubled down on its mistake, albeit with some adjustments. Over the next three months, those big shareholders will be allowed to sell up to a 1% stake--though only with a punitive 15 days' notice. This will merely delay the ultimate reckoning, and keep an overhang of frozen shares over the market.\par \par \uc2 For the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too, the obvious market direction is down, at least against the dollar. China's monetary policy likely needs more loosening, while the U.S. is set to keep raising rates. The dollar is also strong against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China's major trading partners.\par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket unveiled in December may have become the government's yuan-weakening benchmark--a statement posted on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  website Thursday pointed out that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was basically stable against the basket last year. But it remains unclear how closely China intends to track it.\par \par \uc2 All things being equal, a cheaper \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be welcome news for China. After all, owners of European and Japanese equities cheer whenever \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  there fall. But there is a difference between an ordinary decline in a market-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a managed decline in a government-controlled exchange rate like China's.\par \par \uc2 Facing depreciation pressure, China seems to have two choices: Allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall to market-determined level all at once or go for a slow devaluation.\par \par \uc2 But given that the disruptions a rapid fall would cause--to unprepared global markets, diplomatic relations with the West and Chinese companies with unhedged, dollar-denominated debt--it really has only one.\par \par \uc2 The go-slow strategy has costs of its own, though. Repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  drain \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which fell by more than $100 billion in December. Using foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy up yuan also shrinks the domestic money supply, counteracting stimulus efforts. What's more, a slow-motion devaluation creates a one-way bet against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , giving market players an incentive to pull money out of China sooner rather than later.\par \par \uc2 Transitioning from a crawling peg to a freer exchange rate was never going to be easy, but Beijing should have made the move years ago. Waiting until now just forced it to act against a backdrop of slowing growth and a deflating equity bubble.\par \par \uc2 The impression left on investors is that Chinese authorities are out of their depth. Certainly with respect to the stock market, their reputation for incompetence is well-earned. This collapse in confidence will make it harder still for China to engineer a turnaround.\par \par \uc2 Write to Aaron Back at aaron.back@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 07-01-16 1314GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020160107ec170009m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
