\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-Chinese stocks: canary or elephant for world markets this year?\b0\par\par\uc2 1,125 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2016\par 09:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats Wednesday item)\par \par \uc2 * Pessimists see China tumble as international early warning\par \par \uc2 * Optimists say Shanghai stocks should have little global bearing\par \par \uc2 * Markets tend to drop every time Beijing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  - CIO\par \par \uc2 * But \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  stock plunges don't always hit global markets\par \par \uc2 * China's economic clout greater than financial weight\par \par \uc2 By Jamie McGeever\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 6 (Reuters) - Canary in the coal mine or elephant in the room? For investors, either idiom could apply to Chinese stocks in what is already shaping up to be a rocky ride for global markets this year.\par \par \uc2 Pessimists would go for the canary metaphor: the New Year dive that began in Shanghai serves as an early warning of how local events can have repercussions across the world.\par \par \uc2 Optimists would see China as the elephant: impossible to ignore but whose market is so tightly-controlled and largely shut off to foreigners that it shouldn't have such a major bearing on the global investment picture.\par \par \uc2 If market moves this week are any guide, the swings in Shanghai stocks will exert a major sway over world markets this year even though China's equity weighting is relatively small in global terms.\par \par \uc2 The fall in world stocks on Monday, the first trading session of 2016, marked the steepest opening day losses in years for many indexes.\par \par \uc2 Topping the list of investors' reasons to sell was the 7 percent plunge in Shanghai shares earlier that day; this prompted China's first nationwide suspension of trading plus a rapid reaction by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock regulator, hoping to restore calm.\par \par \uc2 Worries about how robust China's financial markets are, the ability of authorities in Beijing to support them and the overall health of the economy are nothing new. They triggered a global market slump late last summer. However, the extent of Monday's fall and the global fallout caught investors off guard.\par \par \uc2 "Markets tend to drop every time Beijing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Investors sense desperation," said Jack Ablin, chief investment officer at BMO Private Bank in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Chinese stocks plunged 45 percent in the three months to August last year. That month the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  underwent a mini devaluation and on Aug. 24 alone the Shanghai equity market tumbled 8.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 This particular fall caused tremors around the world. Wall Street chalked up its biggest loss in four years, with the \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  down as much as 1,000 points at one stage that day.\par \par \uc2 In the past year Shanghai's benchmark composite index has fallen 5 percent or more in a day on 12 occasions. The impact on Wall Street has been broadly negative, with the S&P 500 falling on nine of those days, including a 3.9 percent slide on Aug. 24.\par \par \uc2 But the correlation hasn't been uniform. Four of the S&P's declines were of less than 0.5 percent, and on three days the index was unchanged or higher.\par \par \uc2 For months, investors blamed Chinese markets and China-related factors for any imperfection in the world economy and markets. These concerns gradually receded as the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  prepared for the historic interest rate increase that it finally delivered last month, and as a renewed fall in energy prices intensified the pressure on the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to loosen its monetary policy further.\par \par \uc2 But with the Fed and \uc2 ECB\uc2  unlikely to act again for perhaps several months, attention has switched back to China.\par \par \uc2 "We've come full circle back to China. The level of volatility is concerning although it's encouraging that the U.S. markets finished off their lows on Monday," said Andrew Wilson, managing director at Goldman Sachs Asset Management in London.\par \par \uc2 CARRY THAT WEIGHT\par \par \uc2 Only seven out of 31 strategists in a recent Reuters poll cited China among their top concerns for U.S. stocks in 2016, though five others cited a global recession or slowdown as one of their biggest worries.\par \par \uc2 Part of the problem for investors will be separating what China's economy does from what its stock markets do. There may be some connection between the two over time, but this has been less so in the last couple of years.\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite rose 150 percent in the year to June 2015, during which time economic growth slowed to 7 percent from 7.4 percent - sizeable slippage by China's stellar standards. Despite the tumbles, stocks ended last year up 10 percent, but the economic deceleration shows no sign of easing.\par \par \uc2 "The economic weight of China is much, much bigger than its financial weight," said Luca Paolini, chief strategist at Pictet Asset Management in London.\par \par \uc2 "I wouldn't put Chinese financial market volatility as the main risk for this year but if there's contagion from the Chinese economy to the U.S. and European economies, that's a much more dangerous development," he said.\par \par \uc2 Chinese stocks have a weighting of just 2.5 percent in the \uc2 MSCI\uc2  All Country world index, while the U.S. weighting dwarfs all others with around 53 percent.\par \par \uc2 Economically, the two countries are on a more equal footing. Nominally, the United States accounts for around 25 percent of global GDP and China around 15 percent. But on a purchasing power parity basis, China is the biggest economy in the world with a 17 percent share, edging ahead of America's 16 percent.\par \par \uc2 China's deceleration means global growth is likely to slow too. Any significant undershooting of Beijing's 6.5 percent growth target would put pressure on the U.S. and emerging market economies, as well as commodities and the exchange rates of the Chinese and other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Last year investors pulled an estimated $540 billion out of emerging markets, the first capital outflow from these economies since 1988, according to the Washington-based banking lobby group the \uc2 Institute of International Finance\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Most of that was from China. Some private sector estimates put the outflow closer to $1 trillion, and the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  sold around $500 billion to counter these flows and prevent the yuan from plunging in value.\par \par \uc2 Bruno Taillardat, equity investment director at Unigestion in Geneva, said investors should be highly sensitive to capital flows out of China and further downward pressure on the yuan. He noted the Fed is gradually withdrawing the stimulus it poured into the U.S. economy to counter the financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 "Investors ignored risks in the past because they had to invest all the cash from central banks. But now this is a wake-up call," he said. (Additional reporting by Marcus Howard, Chuck Mikolajczak and Linda Stern in New York; editing by David Stamp) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-7T09:14:42.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160107ec170099p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
