\par\par \b \uc2 Suspected  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  bolsters S.Korea won;  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2 , shares battered by  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  woes, N.Korea tensions\b0\par\par\uc2 462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2016\par 04:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Won, KOSPI fall on China-driven volatility\par     * Won hits four-month intraday low\par \par     SEOUL, Jan 7 (Reuters) - Suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lifted the\par won off a 4-month low struck in early trade, as South\par Korea's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and stocks were battered by the upheaval in the\par markets of its biggest trading partner, China.\par     Investors retreated from emerging markets, like South Korea,\par flocking to safe-haven assets as a sharp fall in Chinese stock\par markets triggered "circuit breakers", and \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par set a sharply lower daily guidance rate for the yuan. \par     Tensions on the Korean Peninsula following the North's\par announcement on Wednesday that it had conducted a nuclear test\par made investors nervous but had no significant impact on trade on\par Thursday.\par     The won was down 0.1 percent to 1,199.3 on the\par dollar as of 0233 GMT, following a recovery induced by suspected\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , dealers said.\par     At its weakest, the won had lost 0.5 percent, striking its\par weakest level since early September, 2015. \par     Dealers doubted how long South Korean authorities could\par cushion the won if the volatility persists.   \par     "China-driven volatility, including the slump in the yuan,\par hit markets this week, keeping investors anxious about their\par next moves," said Park Yuna, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at\par Dongbu Securities.\par     Seoul shares fell sharply for a second straight day as woes\par over world's second biggest economy and geopolitical tensions\par hurt investor sentiment.\par     The South Korea Composite Stock Price Index (KOSPI) \par was down 1 percent at 1,905.57 points. Declining issues\par outnumbered advancing ones by 39-to-10.  \par     Shipbuilders underperformed the broad market,\par falling 3.5 percent. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung Heavy Industries Co Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daewoo Shipbuilding & Marine Engineering Co Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par lost 2.8 and 2 percent, respectively.\par     Shares in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung Electronics Co Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  slid 1\par percent ahead of the release of quarterly report on Friday. \par     Meanwhile, \f28 \uc2 \f2 LG Electronics Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares rose 3.1\par percent to 56,400 won after the company announced it expected to\par sell at least three times as many OLED TV sales this year.\par  \par     Foreign investors were set to extend its selloff streak into\par a 24th consecutive session, dumping a net 82.4 billion Korean\par won ($68.70 million) worth of KOSPI shares near mid-session.\par     March futures on three-year treasury bonds gained\par 0.05 points at 109.74. \par     \par                        0233 GMT    Prev close\par  Dollar/won             1,199.3       1,197.9\par  Yen/won            10.1653/787       10.1234\par  *KTB futures            109.74        109.69\par  KOSPI                 1,905.57      1,925.43\par  * Front-month futures on three-year treasury bonds\par \par \par ($1 = 1,199.4900 won)\par \par  (Reporting by Yeonsoo Kwak; Editing by Simon Cameron-Moore)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-7T04:25:58.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160107ec17003s9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
