\par\par \b \uc2 Australian Dollar Falls, as China Continues to Weaken Yuan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Vera Sprothen   \par \uc2 365 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2016\par 08:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY--The Australian dollar fell Wednesday, as China's ongoing efforts to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to spook investors.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar dropped more than half a cent to as low as US$0.7107, when China's daily exchange-rate fixing showed the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had continued to set the value of the yuan lower against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and has been gradually weakening the yuan since late last year. It also appeared to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the country's stock market Tuesday to prop up plunging stock prices, raising fears among investors worldwide that China's economy is headed for a sharp slowdown and causing a selloff in riskier assets from commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stocks.\par \par \uc2 At 0630 GMT, the Australian dollar was trading at US$0.7105, 1.3% lower than Tuesday around the same time. Stocks across Asia were also down, as volatility remained high.\par \par \uc2 "The volatility we've seen in the Australian dollar over the past few days reflects a lot of the uncertainty about what's going on in China," said \uc2 Paul Bloxham\uc2 , chief economist at HSBC in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 "China is Australia's major trading partner, and when you're nervous about China you sell the Aussie. It's the typical reaction you get in a global risk-off event," Mr. Bloxham said.\par \par \uc2 Economists like him have been surprised about the relative strength in the Australian dollar in recent weeks, which was trading above US$0.73 cents as recently as New Year's Eve, even as commodity prices have continued to decline sharply.\par \par \uc2 Australia's commodity-price index fell 6.0% in Australian dollar terms in December, led by collapsing prices for iron ore and oil, according to the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 . This slide suggests the Australian dollar should be trading at a level somewhere in the mid-60ies U.S. cents, rather than where it is trading now, said Mr. Bloxham.\par \par \uc2 Write to Vera Sprothen at vera.sprothen@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 06-01-16 0735GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020160106ec1600065\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
