\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2 's Stock-Market  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  Postpone Grim Reality -- Heard on the Street\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alex Frangos   \par \uc2 473 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2016\par 05:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Two unequal forces are fighting over China's stock market. And there is likely carnage yet to come.\par \par \uc2 The government on one side is putting up a chaotic fight to prove that there is a floor to share prices. It has squeezed investors' ability to trade, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly by scooping up shares and prevented efficient price discovery generally.\par \par \uc2 On the other side is reality, a bitter set of truths that for China mean a slowing economy, a sliding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , capital outflows, still-high valuations on stocks and too much leverage. Chinese investors are learning yet again that reality tends to prevail in the end.\par \par \uc2 To stop a market landslide for the second day in a row, Beijing injected liquidity into the interbank market and hinted that it would postpone the expiration of a ban on share sales by those who own more than 5% of companies. Direct state buying of shares, as seen frequently last summer, may have turned a 3% drop in the CSI 300 index 45 minutes before the close into a finish up just a quarter of a percent. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prop up the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Investors can hardly exhale. Despite erasing nearly all of 2015's gains, the stock market is probably still overvalued. Strip out the banks, which investors have long ago written off, and the CSI 300 index of stocks trades at around 20 times next 12 months' estimated earnings according to \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 . That is down substantially from a peak near 35 times, but still well above the mid-teens that they traded in the three years before China's bubble inflated starting in the second half of 2014.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Hang Seng A-H Share Premium Index, which measures the difference between the shares of the companies that trade in the unfettered Hong Kong market and not-so-free mainland market, implies the mainland shares are valued at a 38% premium, compared with a five-year average of 8%.\par \par \uc2 There is also still substantial debt embedded in China's equity markets that will have to be unwound eventually. Official margin lending, at 1.2 trillion yuan ($183.6 billion), though down by almost half from the peak, is still more than double where it was before the mania began in mid 2014.\par \par \uc2 So where is the natural bottom? Beijing would probably be better off letting the market find it. Given that little else has changed for the better in terms of corporate earnings and growth, lower than current levels seems likely. But as long as China fights it, the road to reality will be a perilous one.\par \par \uc2 Write to Alex Frangos at alex.frangos@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 06-01-16 0451GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020160106ec160004j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
