\par\par \b \uc2 PBOC\uc2  Expected to Continue Easing to Combat Slowdown -- 1Q  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lingling Wei   \par \uc2 385 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2016\par 20:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 The strength of the U.S. dollar poses a big challenge for \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as it looks to keep easing credit to help revive growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  is widely expected to keep slashing interest rates and banks' required reserves in the first quarter and throughout the year to combat an economic slowdown that is expected to deepen. That effort is likely to face headwinds if the already strong dollar continues to surge and if the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't change the way it manages the yuan.\par \par \uc2 In recent months, the strong dollar has increased pressure on the yuan to depreciate, forcing the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to maintain the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's de facto peg to the greenback. That has meant the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is buying yuan, often from Chinese banks, effectively taking money out of China's financial system at a time when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trying to make more credit available to Chinese businesses and consumers.\par \par \uc2 Now, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is trying to give itself more maneuvering space. It renewed a pledge to decouple the yuan from the dollar and instead will manage the yuan's value based on a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comprising the dollar, euro, yen and 10 other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That would give the yuan room to fall against the dollar, allowing the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  greater freedom to continue to cut rates without having to blow through \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to keep the yuan strong.\par \par \uc2 An initial reason for the peg was to instill confidence in the yuan, so the decoupling carries risks, potentially showing up in more money leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "If capital outflows are huge, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for sure," says \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist Larry Hu at Macquarie Capital Securities. "Otherwise, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  would give the market more freedom in determining the yuan's exchange rate." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Main Policy Rate      Last-Quarter Rate       Current Rate      Outlook \par 1-Year Loan Rate      4.60% Oct. 2, 2015   4.35% Jan. 5, 2016    Down \par 1-Year Deposit Rate   1.75% Oct. 2, 2015   1.50% Jan. 5, 2016    Down \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Lingling Wei at lingling.wei@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 05, 2016 14:00 ET (19:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160105ec15002v0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
