\par\par \b \uc2 China's Stocks Are Hit By 'Panic Selling'\b0\par\par\uc2 1,016 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2016\par 08:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par C1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL 1/5/16) \par    By Chao Deng and Anjani Trivedi in Hong Kong and Mark Magnier in Beijing \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's stock market displayed its global heft Monday, as a sharp selloff in Shanghai ushered in a grim opening day for trading world-wide.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Fast-trading individual investors dumped shares in China on further evidence that its economy was slowing and that Beijing was weakening the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The decline also appeared to be hastened by expectations that support propping up the market could disappear soon, as well as the debut of new circuit breakers.\par \par \uc2 Most of the factors blamed for the selloff aren't new; the surprise was China's impact on the rest of the world. During the market's crash this past summer, it took weeks of selling in China before other markets reacted, while on Monday, just one bad day in the normally volatile market helped send stocks elsewhere tumbling. In the U.S., the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 276.09, or 1.6%, to 17148.94, while the S&P 500 lost 31.28, or 1.5%, 2012.66.\par \par \uc2 "It's quite an unexpected situation today," said Leo Gao, Shanghai-based fund manager at hedge fund Greenwoods Asset Management, of how steep the losses were. "It's more panic selling than anything," he said.\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai market fell 6.9% Monday on the first trading day of the year, its worst day since the height of last summer's market crash. The selloff triggered circuit breakers on their first day in effect, shutting down the market in the early afternoon.\par \par \uc2 People familiar with the matter said early Tuesday that \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is planning to inject 130 billion yuan ($20 billion) in short-term funds to help calm jittery investors after Monday's sharp stock selloff. By deciding to pump the funds into the market, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  is trying to signal to investors that it hasn't changed its easing bias, the people said. The Shanghai Composite opened 3% lower on Tuesday but by lunchtime the index was up 0.4%.\par \par \uc2 The most obvious factor behind Monday's selloff was a private index of manufacturing activity in China, which showed that business had slowed for the 10th consecutive month for the country's steelmakers, shipbuilders and other industries.\par \par \uc2 The news adds to downbeat data from China, which is expected to disclose this month whether it hit its growth target of about 7% for 2015. That would be the country's slowest growth in 25 years, and economists say it could set an even-lower target of around 6.5% for 2016.\par \par \uc2 Another worry was China's falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which crossed a key technical threshold as it hit another nearly five-year low. The expectation that the yuan will weaken further is one factor causing Chinese to send cash overseas, which itself causes the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline to deepen.\par \par \uc2 Monday's decline in the yuan started after \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  guided the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaker in the morning, setting the midpoint for the day's trading range at 6.5032 yuan, its weakest level since 2011. China lets the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade 2% above or below that level in its onshore market.\par \par \uc2 Although the yuan has been weakening steadily for months, traders say they suspect \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets to slow the decline -- something that didn't appear to happen Monday.\par \par \uc2 "There's been a lot of surprise in the market that China hasn't slowed this decline [in the yuan] or come in more aggressively," said Mitul Kotecha, head of rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy in Asia at \uc2 Barclays\uc2 , referring to efforts to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling dollars.\par \par \uc2 Another pressure point on the market Monday: concern that one of the bailout measures imposed by the government during the crash is set to expire on Friday, which could trigger more selling. Under that effort, one of several frantic moves by Beijing to halt the August selling, big shareholders were banned from selling stock for six months. Most analysts expect Beijing to extend the ban if the market keeps falling.\par \par \uc2 China's market is dominated by fast-trading individual investors who tend to buy when shares are rising, especially if they believe the government wants the market to go up, and sell when stocks are falling. That appeared to be the case Monday and might have been hastened by the knowledge that the market could shut down if circuit breakers, announced in December, were tripped.\par \par \uc2 The circuit breakers first kicked in right after the market's lunch break, shutting down trading for 15 minutes after a 5% drop. But as soon as shares started trading again, the market plummeted, hitting a 7% decline in just a few minutes. Trading was shut for the rest of the day.\par \par \uc2 "The circuit-breaker system actually creates a downward spiral" as more investors get nervous about trying to get out before others, said Hao Hong, managing director at Bank of Communications Co. "Having this so-called system in place is actually making the selling worse."\par \par \uc2 Early Tuesday, China's stock regulator said the circuit-breaker mechanism cooled down the market and had a positive role in protecting investor interest during Monday's trading.\par \par \uc2 Monday marked a fresh round of volatility for Chinese markets, which have rebounded by more than 20% from their lows of the summer, when heavy selling eventually spurred volatility around the globe. Over 1,200 stocks on the Shanghai and Shenzhen market, or more than 42% of firms trading, fell by the 10% daily downward limit set by regulators, according to Wind Information.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Lingling Wei and Yifan Xie contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This article was corrected on January 5, 2016 at 6:00 p.m. ET because a reference to the Dow Jones Industrial Average's Monday close of 17148.94 omitted the last digit.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 05, 2016 02:43 ET (07:43 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160105ec15000jd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
