\par\par \b \uc2 German bond yields stabilise after Chinese  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 503 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2016\par 17:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes measures to halt \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stock slide\par \par \uc2 * But analysts doubt they will solve China's problems\par \par \uc2 * Inflation falls short of forecasts\par \par \uc2 * Catalan yields hit three-week low as separatists quarrel (Updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By John Geddie\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 5 (Reuters) - A decline in German bond yields levelled out on Tuesday after China took measures to halt a slide in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock market, offseting most of the impact of below-forecast euro zone inflation data.\par \par \uc2 German yields dipped 1 basis point (bps) to 0.56 percent, stabilising after Monday's 9-bps slide, when investors were looking for a safe haven amid geopolitical tensions and doubts about the health of China's economy.\par \par \uc2 Beijing injected liquidity into domestic markets, set the value of its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher than many had expected and said it was studying rules to regulate share sales by major holders. There were also reports of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets by state-owned funds to try and halt equity rout.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said those actions looked like only a temporary fix after Monday's 7-percent drop in Chinese shares..\par \par \uc2 "China's actions are certainly positive at the margin ... but overall the risk is that it is interpreted as a signal of weakness that these ongoing struggles to stabilise the market by the authorities aren't really bearing fruit," \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  strategist Michael Leister said.\par \par \uc2 The measures pushed Chinese shares up 0.3 percent on Tuesday. Euro zone stocks also rose 0.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 Lower-rated debt in Spain and Italy outperformed the German benchmark, with yields down 3-5 bps at 1.70 and 1.51 percent, respectively.\par \par \uc2 In Catalonia, one of Spain's richest regions, bond yields fell to a three-week low as a local separatist coalition fractured, potentially leading to new elections and delaying independence plans.\par \par \uc2 Spain's acting Prime Minister Mariano Rajoy said on Tuesday that a new regional election in Catalonia was inevitable.\par \par \uc2 INFLATION FRUSTRATION\par \par \uc2 Euro zone inflation data showed no sign that base effects from last year's slide in oil prices had faded away, creating more headaches for the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That kept up the downward pressure on yields.\par \par \uc2 Headline inflation was unchanged at 0.2 percent in December, missing expectations for a rise to 0.3 percent and far short of the bank's target of close to 2 percent. The core inflation rate - which strips out energy costs - fell to 0.8 percent from November's 0.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Yields are going to find it difficult to rise on the back of this (data) clearly," \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  strategist Orlando Green said.\par \par \uc2 Heightened tensions in the Middle East are also keeping investors cautious. Saudi Arabia cut diplomatic ties with Iran on Sunday after protesters stormed its embassy. The attack followed the Saudi execution of a prominent Shi'ite cleric, Nimr al-Nimr, which outraged predominately Shi'ite Iran. (Additional reporting by Marius Zaharia; Editing by Louise Ireland) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-5T17:12:13.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160105ec1500jx2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
