\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 198 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2016\par 12:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Eurozone Inflation Remains Stubbornly Low\par \par \uc2 Consumer prices in the eurozone rose at an unchanged and sluggish pace in December, a fresh sign that it is proving more difficult for the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost inflation than policy makers had expected.\par \par \uc2 PBOC\uc2  Injects $20 Billion to Calm Nervous Investors\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  injected 130 billion yuan ($19.9 billion) in short-term funds into the country's financial system, according to a statement, in an effort to help calm jittery investors after Monday's sharp stock selloff.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Steps In to Prop Up Yuan\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Tuesday to prop up a weakening yuan, as Beijing adopted a broad suite of measures to stem a selloff in the country's stock market.\par \par \uc2 Beijing Muddles Markets With Mixed Signals\par \par \uc2 After spooking global investors by weakening the yuan to an important psychological level and damping hopes for aggressive measures to spur growth, Beijing policy makers Tuesday sought to calm them by injecting nearly $20 billion into the financial system.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 05, 2016 06:30 ET (11:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160105ec150015e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
