\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -China market stabilisation efforts save Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  for now\b0\par\par\uc2 789 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2016\par 06:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Yuan gains with c.bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suspected\par     * Rupiah up on inflows ahead of Indonesia bond auction\par     * Ringgit rises on stocks, leveraged funds\par     * Won turns up; S.Korea to take actions if needed\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Jan 5 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par rebounded on Tuesday as China took steps to restore calm,\par including through suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , after\par a plunge in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's share markets the previous day forced\par emerging market investors into retreat.\par     Strong doubts remained over how long the uneasy calm would\par last, as there are mounting concerns over the economic slowdown.\par     "I still see downside risks from China where growth is\par slowing with no prospect of stabilisation in the manufacturing\par sector. More importantly, the CNY and CNH have weakened to\par levels, not anticipated by many - too much, too fast," said\par Nordea Markets' senior analyst Amy Yuan Zhuang in Singapore,\par referring to onshore and offshore yuan.\par     "The key question, which is hard to predict at the moment\par with vague political signal, is whether Beijing will continue\par weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . If so, then Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could feel the\par pressure."\par     The onshore Chinese yuan rose on Tuesday as the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set its daily guidance rate slightly firmer than\par many had expected. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authority was also\par suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through state-run banks to support the\par renminbi, traders said. \par     To further aid sentiment, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par injected 130 billion yuan ($19.9 billion) into money markets - \par the largest daily liquidity injection since Sept. 8.\par  \par     Those measures helped both mainland China stocks and other\par riskier assets in Asia, bolstering some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the\par region.\par     Indonesia's rupiah led gains, with inflows seen \par ahead of a government bond sale later in the day. Malaysia's\par ringgit rose on higher local stocks.\par     The South Korean won turned higher on growing\par caution over possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par authorities.\par     \par     RUPIAH\par     The rupiah started the day weaker but turned firmer as\par Jakarta stocks jumped more than 1 percent, outperforming\par regional peers.\par     The Indonesian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  extended gains on capital inflows\par ahead of the government's bond auction with an indicative target\par of 12 trillion rupiah ($868.6 million) later in the day.\par     A \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader in Jakarta said the government could sell\par as much as 18 trillion rupiah.\par     Easing inflation and a shrinking current account deficit\par have recently drawn foreign investors to seek higher yields from\par Indonesia's bonds. In December, the annual inflation rate cooled\par to the lowest in six years. \par     \par     RINGGIT\par     The ringgit rose as Malaysian equities also advanced over 1\par percent.\par     Leveraged funds bought the Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par non-deliverable forwards markets.\par     The ringgit also found support from demand against the\par neighbouring Singapore dollar.\par     \par     WON\par     The won earlier skidded to 1,192.1 per dollar, its weakest\par since Sept. 30, tracking weakness in the offshore yuan.\par     The South Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reversed initial losses as the\par onshore renminbi rose on suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par     Caution also grew over possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by South Korea\par as the authorities were on Monday spotted in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par to stem the won's weakness.\par     "A turnaround in Chinese markets and suspected dollar\par supplies from the authorities caused stop-loss dollar selling,"\par said a South Korean bank trader in Seoul.\par     A senor \f28 \uc2 \f2 South Korean finance ministry\f28 \uc2 \f2  official said on\par Tuesday the government will take action to stabilise markets if\par needed. \par         \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0500 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       119.52         119.46       -0.05\par   Sing dlr        1.4237         1.4248       +0.08\par   Taiwan dlr      33.061         33.236       +0.53\par   Korean won     1185.50        1187.70       +0.19\par   Baht             36.12          36.15       +0.07\par   Peso             47.02          47.12       +0.21\par   Rupiah        13815.00       13910.00       +0.69\par   Rupee            66.44          66.61       +0.25\par   Ringgit         4.3370         4.3490       +0.28\par   Yuan            6.5197         6.5338       +0.22\par  \par   Change so far in 2016\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       119.52         120.30       +0.65\par   Sing dlr        1.4237         1.4177       -0.42\par   Taiwan dlr      33.061         33.066       +0.02\par   Korean won     1185.50        1172.50       -1.10\par   Baht             36.12          36.00       -0.33\par   Peso             47.02          47.06       +0.09\par   Rupiah        13815.00       13785.00       -0.22\par   Rupee            66.44          66.15       -0.44\par   Ringgit         4.3370         4.2935       -1.00\par   Yuan            6.5197         6.4936       -0.40\par  \par  (Additional reporting by Yena Park in SEOUL and IFR Markets'\par Catherine Tan; Editing by Simon Cameron-Moore)\f28 \par \par \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-5T06:49:23.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160105ec15006px\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 European Markets Seen Up Despite China Concerns\b0\par\par\uc2 485 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2016\par \uc2 RTT News\par \uc2 RTTNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2016 NoticiasFinancieras. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (RTTNews) - European stocks are seen opening higher on Tuesday as risk sentiment improved somewhat on expectations for more easing measures from \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  said on Monday it had extended 100 billion yuan ($15.3 billion) in loans to 13 financial institutions, in an effort to calm jittery investors after a sell-off triggered the first trading halt on China's mainland stock exchanges the previous day.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize the sliding yuan. However, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Shanghai Composite index was down more than 3 percent in volatile trade.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's Kospi average was moving up 0.6 percent after South Korea downplayed the impact of China's stock market crash on its domestic financial market, saying stability will soon return. Japan's Nikkei index was losing 0.4 percent after plunging 3.1 percent the previous day.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices rebounded in Asian deals after a volatile session the previous day. The Middle East slid dangerously closer to regional conflict after a number of Saudi Arabia's allies joined diplomatic action against Iran in protest at the execution of Sheikh Nimr al-Nimr.\par \par \uc2 Copper eased while gold extended overnight gains driven by safe-haven buying. The euro held little changed, a day after German inflation figures underlined the pervasiveness of weak price pressures across the Eurozone.\par \par \uc2 It's a light day on the economic front, with investors awaiting inflation figures from the euro area and unemployment data from Germany for further direction.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, following an extraordinary policy meeting, the \uc2 Swedish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would move more swiftly in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem the rise of the krona.\par \par \uc2 In corporate news, \uc2 Groupama\uc2 , France-based mutual insurance company, and Orange said they have entered into exclusive negotiations with a view to working in partnership to develop a new banking model.\par \par \uc2 BioM\u233\'00\'E9rieux\uc2 , an in vitro diagnostics provider, announced the acquisition of Applied Maths, a privately-held developer of software solutions for the biosciences.\par \par \uc2 European stocks sold off sharply on Monday as investors fretted about China's slowing growth. Upbeat Eurozone manufacturing data did little to lift investor sentiment. The German DAX plunged 4.3 percent, France's CAC 40 lost 2.5 percent and the U.K.'s FTSE 100 shed 2.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks fell sharply on the first day of trade for the year on Monday, weighed down by steep declines in Chinese stocks, increased tensions in the Middle East and disappointing U.S. data on manufacturing activity and construction spending.\par \par \uc2 The Dow tumbled 1.6 percent and the S&P 500 dropped 1.5 percent, while the tech-heavy \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  slumped 2.1 percent to end at its lowest level in well over two months.\par \par \uc2 For comments and feedback: contact editorial@rttnews.com\par \par \uc2 Copyright(c) 2016 RTTNews.com. All Rights Reserved\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTTNEW0020160105ec15001rx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
