\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korea shares recover as China stocks stabilise; won slightly up\b0\par\par\uc2 411 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2016\par 04:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Shares reverse early losses; foreigners sell for 22nd day\par     * Won held in tight range in the morning trade\par \par     By Yeonsoo Kwak\par     SEOUL, Jan 5 (Reuters) - South Korean shares reversed\par initial losses and rose on Tuesday morning, following a rebound\par in China stocks, but investors remained cautious as support for\par local stocks could be short-lived in a volatile trading\par environment.  \par     The Korea Composite Stock Price Index (KOSPI) was up\par 0.8 percent at 1,934.38 points as of 0233 GMT. \par     A \f28 \uc2 \f2 South Korean finance ministry\f28 \uc2 \f2  official said on Tuesday the\par government will take action to stabilise markets if needed,\par following a meeting of ministry officials a day after a steep\par plunge in Chinese stocks set off falls on the local bourse.\par  \par     Lee Jae-man, a stock analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hana Daetoo Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said\par investors had initially been panic-stricken by the collapse in\par China stocks, but the situation now appears not to be as bad as\par first thought. \par     Lee added that shocks in the Chinese markets would subside\par over time as negative factors such as big "national team"\par institutions selling stocks and companies launching new IPOs had\par been in effect for some time. \par     Shares in recently-battered brokerages advanced, with\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung Securities Co Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mirae Asset Securities\par Co Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  rising 2.6 percent and 4.6 percent,\par respectively.\par     Steelmaker POSCO rose 2.7 percent, and memory\par chip maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 SK Hynix Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2  gained 2.5 percent.  \par     Foreigners were expected to extend a 21-straight day bout of\par net selling, offloading a net 18.8 billion won ($15.87 million)\par worth of KOSPI shares on the main board by midday. \par     The South Korean won rebounded slightly as the yuan\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  firmed after suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , while \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's weak factory activity surveys seemed not to\par affect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market for too long.\par     The won was quoted up 0.2 percent at 1,185.6 on\par the dollar, compared with the previous onshore close of 1,187.7.\par     March futures on three-year treasury bonds edged\par down 0.02 points at 109.72. \par     \par                        0233 GMT    Prev close\par  Dollar/won             1,185.6       1,187.7\par  Yen/won             9.9114/280        9.9561\par  *KTB futures            109.72        109.74\par  KOSPI                 1,934.38      1,918.76\par  * Front-month futures on three-year treasury bonds\par \par  (Reporting by Yeonsoo Kwak; Editing by Eric Meijer)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-5T04:10:46.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160105ec15003xp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
