\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2 's yuan firms on suspected  \highlight22\uc2 interventions \highlight\uc2  but more weakness seen ahead\b0\par\par\uc2 596 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2016\par 04:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par HONG KONG, Jan 5 (Reuters) - China's onshore yuan firmed\par against the dollar on Tuesday after suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , a day after a slide in the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and\par stock markets sparked a panicky global sell-off.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the midpoint rate \par at 6.5169 per dollar prior to the market open, weaker than the\par previous fix of 6.5032. It was the weakest level since April\par 2011.\par     In the spot market, the yuan strengthened\par immediately after the opening on Tuesday and was changing hands\par at 6.5198 near midday, 140 pips stronger than the previous close\par and 0.04 percent weaker than the midpoint. \par     The spot rate is allowed to trade with a range 2 percent\par above or below the official fixing on any given day.\par     "It's quite obvious that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par the market via big Chinese banks in the morning and trading was\par very active," said a trader at a Chinese bank in Shanghai.\par     Despite the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the trader said his strategy was\par to continue shorting the yuan given China's weak economic\par fundamentals.\par     The trader expected the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to fall to 6.6 per\par dollar by the end of the year.\par     The yuan and China's stock markets slumped on Monday as\par surveys showed its factory activity continued to contract,\par reviving fears of a global economic slowdown. \par     Onshore yuan closed at 6.5338 on Monday.\par     "State-owned banks were offering dollar liquidity around\par 6.52 per dollar," said a Shanghai trader at a major European\par bank. "They were apparently trading on behalf of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  to\par help control the pace of yuan depreciation."\par     In the offshore market, where the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  usually takes\par a hands-off attitude, the yuan hit 6.6446 in early trade, the\par lowest in more than four years. It traded 1.71 percent weaker\par than the onshore spot at 6.6335 per dollar near midday. \par     Offshore one-year non-deliverable forwards contracts\par (NDFs), considered the best available proxy for\par forward-looking market expectations of the yuan's value, traded\par at 6.853, or 4.90 percent weaker than the midpoint.\par     One-year NDFs are settled against the midpoint, not the spot\par rate, and now that the trading band has been widened to 2\par percent in either direction, corporates are much warier of using\par the NDF to hedge given the basis risk inherent in them.\par     As a result the market has lost liquidity in recent years\par and has frequently proven an unreliable measure of market\par sentiment.\par \par     The yuan market at a glance: \par     \par     ONSHORE:\par  Item                        Current  Previous  Change\par  PBOC midpoint               6.5169   6.5032    -0.21%\par  Spot yuan                   6.5198   6.5338    0.21%\par  Divergence from midpoint*   0.04%              \par  Spot change ytd                                -4.84%\par  Spot change since 2005 revaluation             26.94%\par  *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET  \par  Instrument                   Current   Difference\par                                         from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan        *  6.6335    -1.71%\par  Offshore non-deliverable     6.853     -4.90%\par  forwards               **              \par  *Premium for offshore spot over onshore \par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par . \par \par     \par  (Reporting by Michelle Chen and Lu Jianxin; Additional\par reporting by Shanghai Newroom; Editing by Kim Coghill)\f28 \par \par \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-5T04:08:38.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160105ec15003pk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
