\par\par \b \uc2 China Market Plunge Has Investors Wondering About More Turmoil\b0\par\par\uc2 1,186 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Januar 2016\par 16:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Chao Deng,Anjani Trivedi in Hong Kong and Mark Magnier in Beijing \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's economic troubles pounded Asian and global markets on the first trading day of the year, causing shares to tumble and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken and leaving investors worried about a continuation of last year's market turmoil.\f28 \par \par \uc2 In mainland China, the Shanghai market's benchmark index fell 6.9% and smaller Shenzhen's dropped 8.2%, posting declines so steep that more than 40% of listed companies hit their daily downward trading limits, and authorities halted trading midday. Benchmark stock indexes slid 3.1% in Japan, 2.2% in South Korea and 2.7% in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Pan-European benchmark Stoxx Europe 600 fell 2.6%. Just after markets opened in the U.S., the Dow Jones Industrial Average tumbled 343 points, or 2%, and the S&P 500 was off 1.4%.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan fell to its weakest level versus the dollar in nearly five years, pushing down \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from South Korea's won to the Malaysian ringgit, as fears escalated that the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's accelerating slide would drag down the economies of its trading partners.\par \par \uc2 Investors and traders blamed concerns about China's massive economy, whose decelerating growth and slackening demand has pummeled markets for commodities and industrial exports and led a host of countries around the globe to downgrade their growth outlooks.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, one of the first Chinese economic data points to be released in 2016--a closely watched private index of manufacturing--suggested the country is starting off the year on shaky ground, fueling investor concern the slumping industrial sector may be worse off than expected.\par \par \uc2 The yuan also weakened swiftly Monday, with no sign that authorities would step in to prop it up as they often have in recent months.\par \par \uc2 Some investors also were worrying about the expected lifting of a six-month ban Friday that had kept big shareholders from unloading stocks. Chinese regulators implemented the ban during the summer in an attempt to halt a market selloff.\par \par \uc2 "A large chunk of the market still believes that China is doing much better than it really is," said Lin-Jing Leong, a bond-fund manager at Aberdeen Asset Management, which manages $429.7 billion in assets. When evidence that things aren't looking so good surfaces, "panic emerges," she said.\par \par \uc2 Monday marked a fresh bout of jitters for Chinese markets, following a rout during the summer where heavy selling triggered volatility around the globe. Monday's losses wiped out the bulk of Shanghai's 9.4% gains for 2015, putting the benchmark up only 1.9% over the past 12 months. They also triggered the brand-new circuit-breaker rules Chinese authorities put in place this year, to halt the kinds of wild market swings seen last year.\par \par \uc2 Market watchers say they aren't expecting the current fall to develop into a full-fledged repeat of last year's plunge. A big difference this time is that investors have cut back on borrowing money to buy stocks, or margin loans. During the summer, local investors borrowed money from Chinese brokerages, which drove shares sharply up, and down, as investors sold stakes to repay their brokers.\par \par \uc2 Since then, official margin loans in China's mainland market have fallen more than 49% from a peak of 2.3 trillion yuan in June. Authorities also clamped down on loans from unofficial "shadow" lenders and took aggressive steps to calm the market, from cracking down on short sellers to tasking government funds to buy shares.\par \par \uc2 More recently, authorities eased up on some of those measures, including a freeze on public listings, which restarted in December--a sign many investors interpreted as officials' vote of confidence in the stock market's stability.\par \par \uc2 "It's quite an unexpected situation today," said Leo Gao, Shanghai-based fund manager at hedge fund Greenwoods Asset Management, of how steep the losses were. "It's more panic selling than anything," he said.\par \par \uc2 The investor jitters were triggered in part by data Monday released by Caixin Media Co. suggesting that manufacturing in China contracted for the 10th consecutive month. In addition, indexes that track production, new orders and new export orders all declined. That followed the release of China's official purchasing managers index on New Year's Day, which showed a measure of factory-floor conditions edging up slightly but still indicating that overall activity is shrinking.\par \par \uc2 The news adds to worrying data from China, which is expected to reveal later this month whether it hit its growth target of about 7% for 2015. That would be the country's slowest growth in 25 years, and economists say it could set an even-lower target of around 6.5% for 2016.\par \par \uc2 "Business is not good," said Chen Ming, a marketing assistant with Zhuhai Bo Yen Houseware Manufactory Ltd. in southern Guangdong province, which exports stone and metal garden furniture to Europe, the U.S. and Southeast Asia. "The orders are dropping. Many factories are shutting down. Things are getting tougher."\par \par \uc2 The weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --the yuan depreciated 2% against the dollar in December--hasn't helped their business because customers ask for discounts whenever it softens, she said.\par \par \uc2 Monday's decline in the yuan started after \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  guided the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaker in the morning, setting the midpoint for the day's trading range at 6.5032 yuan, its weakest level since 2011. China lets the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade 2% above or below that level in its onshore market.\par \par \uc2 Although the yuan has been weakening steadily for months, traders say they suspect \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets to slow the decline--something that didn't appear to happen Monday.\par \par \uc2 "There's been a lot of surprise in the market that China hasn't slowed this decline [in the yuan] or come in more aggressively," said Mitul Kotecha, head of rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy in Asia at \uc2 Barclays\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The offshore and onshore yuan traded at their weakest levels since April 2011, with the onshore yuan as weak as 6.5381 to one U.S. dollar in late trade Monday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia slid across the board against the U.S. dollar, with South Korea's won down more than 1.4%, the Malaysian ringgit down 1.2% and the Taiwanese dollar down 0.9%, amid fears the Chinese yuan's accelerating slide would drag down these economies.\par \par \uc2 Write to Anjani Trivedi at anjani.trivedi@wsj.com and Mark Magnier at mark.magnier@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Corrections and Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This item was corrected at 04:38 GMT. The original incorrectly stated loans have fallen 17% in the 12th paragraph.\par \par \uc2 4 Jan 2016 23:51 ET  Correction to China Market Plunge Story on Monday\par \par \uc2 Margin loans in China have fallen 49% from their peak in June. "China Market Plunge Has Investors Wondering About More Turmoil," at 1459 GMT, incorrectly stated loans have fallen 17% in the 12th paragraph. (Jan. 5)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\p