\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan closes at 4-1/2 year low; how far will c.bank let it go?\b0\par\par\uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2015\par 10:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Yuan slips to 6.4553, breaching post-devaluation level\par \par \uc2 * Yuan posts biggest weekly loss since August\par \par \uc2 * C.bank suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 6.45/dollar\par \par \uc2 * Some traders see yuan at 6.50/dollar at year-end\par \par \uc2 By Lu Jianxin and Pete Sweeney\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Dec 11 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed at its lowest level in nearly four and 1/2 years on Friday, raising questions over how far Beijing will let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken.\par \par \uc2 The yuan fell 0.3 percent on the day, taking its losses this year to nearly 4 percent, but there were no signs of broader global market panic like that which China triggered in mid-August when it surprisingly devalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Friday's lowest point for the yuan was 6.4564 to the dollar, in the late afternoon, before it closed at 6.4553.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the yuan fell 0.8 percent, its biggest loss since the devaluation and a sixth straight week of declines - the longest such streak since December 2005, according to \uc2 Thomson Reuters\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 After the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday morning breached a low hit in August after Beijing's devaluation, major state banks were suspected of entering the market to help stabilise for the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , traders said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been conspicuously absent from markets in recent sessions, leading many traders to believe it is content to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to depreciate gradually in the face of the U.S. dollar's relentless rise.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  will move more aggressively from a managed float to a more market based \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, meaning little to no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which should lead to further weakness," predicted Stephen Innes, senior trader at \uc2 OANDA\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 A senior trader at a Chinese commercial bank in Shanghai said the government "has internal targets for the yuan, though we're not certain of the ranges. But the market generally agrees 6.50 may be the upper limit the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is willing to permit this year."\par \par \uc2 NO PANIC THIS TIME\par \par \uc2 August's depreciation sparked strong \uc2 PBOC\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though it said the devaluation was part of moves to let the yuan trade more freely.\par \par \uc2 Unlike in August, when they were stunned by devaluation, traders in China were calm about the yuan's recent slide.\par \par \uc2 "Trading was quite normal, unlike a flurry of sales in August," said a dealer at a European bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 "The yuan is weakening all this week, but the market was less panic than in mid-August, leaving the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in no hurry to take action."\par \par \uc2 Prior to Friday's market opening, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  set its official yuan midpoint rate t 6.4358 per dollar, its weakest level since Aug. 5, 2011.\par \par \uc2 The weak yuan was one factor hurting Chinese stock markets on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "If the yuan continues to depreciate, that's negative to stocks as well, because it means investors are not confident about China's economic restructuring," said Linus Yip, chief strategist at First Shanghai Securities.\par \par \uc2 LINK WITH SDR MOVE?\par \par \uc2 The yuan started weakening a few days after the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's Nov. 30 announcement that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would enter its Special Drawing Rights (SDR) basket, a milestone in China's integration into global finance.\par \par \uc2 Hours after the announcement, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  said there was no basis for the yuan to continue to devalue, and made clear \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basically stable as it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when there were abnormal movements.\par \par \uc2 Markets were rife with speculation that Beijing would let the yuan depreciate after the SDR inclusion, and the performance this week appears to justify that view, some traders said.\par \par \uc2 However, other traders suspect the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is increasing the volatility of yuan trading, letting it depreciate before the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's U.S. rate decision next week, and will then guide it to appreciate afterwards.\par \par \uc2 Speculators have been burnt many times by the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's temporary tolerance of sharp yuan movements in one direction, followed by a hard strike back in the opposite direction.\par \par \uc2 "If the \uc2 PBOC\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the yuan's value, the lower limit is likely to be 6.4 per dollar, leaving the yuan to move in a wide range of 1,000 pips," said a dealer at another Chinese commercial bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 In the offshore market, the yuan was quoted at 6.5356 per dollar, having lost 1.4 percent so far this week. The spread between onshore and offshore yuan widened to more than 800 pips. (Additional reporting by Michelle Chen in Hong Kong and Shanghai Newsroom; Editing by Richard Borsuk) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-12-11T10:55:48.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020151211ebcb00ci5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
