\par\par \b \uc2 METALS-London copper climbs in quiet trade ahead of Fed\b0\par\par\uc2 609 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2015\par 05:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * China aluminium smelters to close 5 mln T capacity this year\par \par \uc2 * Most of this capacity is old, already factored in - analysts\par \par \uc2 * Coming Up: U.S. Retail sales Nov at 1330 GMT (Adds comment, detail, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Melanie Burton\par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE, Dec 11 (Reuters) - London copper climbed on Friday and was set to close the week little changed as demand from China wanes and as traders cut risk ahead of a \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  meeting next week.\par \par \uc2 Fed Chair \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  will next week decide whether to raise interest rates for the first time in a decade and how to assure markets on the likely path of future rate policy.\par \par \uc2 The dollar, which has steadily marched higher this year has exerted downwards pressure on commodities, which become more expensive for buyers paying with other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar climbs.\par \par \uc2 But analysts flagged a dollar turnaorund if the Fed raises rates, helping metals recover.\par \par \uc2 "I think metals will get a bit of a bounce going into the Fed meeting -- too many people all lined up on the short side and so we could see a counter move within a down market," said analyst \uc2 Ed Meir\uc2  of INTL FC Stone in New York.\par \par \uc2 "It will be short-lived though."\par \par \uc2 Three-month copper on the London Metal Exchange climbed by 0.9 percent to $4,629 a tonne by 0427 GMT, adding to a small gain from the previous session. Prices have been stuck in a rut between $4,450 and $4,650 since late November, when they slumped to more than a six-year low.\par \par \uc2 Shanghai Futures Exchange\uc2  copper edged up by 0.4 percent to 35,260 yuan ($5,467) a tonne.\par \par \uc2 Aluminium prices were little changed, even as China's top smelters said they would cut capacity by 500,000 tonnes by year-end, taking total capacity cuts for 2015 to just under 5 million tonnes as they strive to whittle down a domestic supply glut.\par \par \uc2 The smelters also said they would not open any new capacity for at least one year.\par \par \uc2 But analysts said around 3.5 million tonnes of expensive, outdated capacity had already been marked for closure this year, and the figure didn't include new capacity.\par \par \uc2 "They can stop all the new smelter capacity they like. Nobody is even remotely thinking about building capacity with prices where they are," said analyst Paul Adkins of China-based consultancy AZ China.\par \par \uc2 LME nickel prices climbed 1.2 percent as investors covered short positions in thin trade. Prices were still facing a weekly fall of more than 4 percent amid persistently slack stainless steel demand and near record oversupply. ShFE nickel fell 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 China's yuan closed down against the dollar for the fifth session on Thursday, while the offshore market accelerated away from the onshore rate, stirring speculation over whether the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap any further losses.\par \par \uc2 Aluminium producer \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Hongqiao Group said on Thursday it would cut annual capacity by 250,000 tonnes immediately, the latest smelter to pledge to curb supplies as the loss making industry combats record low local prices. \par \par \uc2 \f2               \par               \par               \par               \par PRICES\par               \par Three month LME copper\par               \par Most active ShFE copper\par               \par Three month LME aluminium\par               \par Most active ShFE aluminium\par              