\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-China  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves plung to lowest level since Feb 2013\b0\par\par\uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 2015\par 11:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fall to $3.44 trillion in Nov               \par \par \uc2 * Drop in Nov reserves sharpest since August               \par \par \uc2 * Fund outflows put pressure on c.bank to support yuan   (Adds background, milestones, market performance, analyst comment)               \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Dec 7 (Reuters) - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's largest, fell by $87.2 billion in November to $3.44 trillion, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Monday, the lowest level since February 2013 and the third largest monthly drop on record.               \par \par \uc2 Analysts blamed the fall partly the dollar's rally during November, which reduced the value of non-dollar reserves, and partly on \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling dollars to support the yuan.               \par \par \uc2 The onshore yuan is down over 3 percent so far this year, and remains under pressure as investors expect U.S. interest rates to be increased for the first time in around a decade later this month.               \par \par \uc2 The fall in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves was the biggest since a record monthly drop of $93.9 billion in August. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have declined for the last five quarters and posted a record quarterly fall in the third quarter.               \par \par \uc2 "The pick-up in capital outflows appears to have been predominately driven by increased expectations for renminbi (yuan) depreciation," Julian Evans-Pritchard at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  said in a note.               \par \par \uc2 "A rise in offshore interest rates due to the increased likelihood of a December Fed rate hike will also have added to outflow pressures."               \par \par \uc2 China's surprise devaluation of the yuan on Aug. 11 fueled a wave of capital outflows on fears the world's second-largest economy might be slowing more sharply than thought, and on worries of a possible interest rate rise by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 The yuan's depreciation has been even more marked in the offshore maket, where it has lost over 4 percent this year.               \par \par \uc2 Trading at 6.47 per yuan dollar, the offshore rate  is at its widest discount to the onshore closing rate since September, indicating enduring bearishness.               \par \par \uc2 Zhou Hao, economist at Commerzbank in Singapore, wrote in a reaserch note that the Peoples Bank of China has switched strategies to manage a softening to the yuan-dollar rate instead of trying to halt it completely.               \par \par \uc2 "It seems that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has changed its strategy in market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," wrote Zhou.               \par \par \uc2 "Recall what happened in August and September: USD-CNY was stable despite soaring trading volumes, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold \uc2 USD\uc2  aggressively to safeguard its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 "Afterwards, USD-CNY trading volume illustrates a clear downward trend since September, and CNY gradually weakens in tandem. The difference shows that a 'managed depreciation' in CNY is acceptable from Chinese authorities' perspective."               \par \par \uc2 For its part the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly said it sees no reason for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to depreciate further.               \par \par \uc2 The value of China's gold reserves stood at $59.52 billion at the end of November, down from $63.26 billion at the end of October, the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 said on its website.               \par \par \uc2 China's \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) reserve position was at $4.60 billion, down from $4.64 billion the previous month. It held $10.18 billion of \uc2 IMF\uc2  Special Drawing Rights at the end of last month, compared with $10.36 billion at the end of October.               \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in July shifted to reporting its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves on a monthly basis after adopting the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's Special Data Dissemination Standard (SDDS). The bank had previously released the data on a quarterly basis.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-12-7T11:46:28.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020151207ebc700bd1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
