\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Emerging stocks set for monthly loss, Turkey shrugs off sanctions\b0\par\par\uc2 672 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2015\par 12:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Turkish lira, stocks shrug off Moscow sanctions               \par \par \uc2 * Emerging stocks on track for 3.6 percent loss in November               \par \par \uc2 * Yuan jumps in offshore trade ahead of \uc2 IMF\uc2  decision               \par \par \uc2 By Karin Strohecker               \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 30 (Reuters) - Emerging stocks ended the month on a sour note on Monday, down more than 3 percent on the month, although Turkish assets appeared unfazed by Moscow's decision to slap sanctions on Ankara over last week's downing of a fighter jet.               \par \par \uc2 MSCI\uc2 's broadest emerging market index slipped 1 percent and was set to end November in the red - chalking up losses for six out of the past seven months.               \par \par \uc2 China mainland stocks swung wildly in and out of negative territory, fraying investors' nerves in a highly volatile session before ending flat after Friday's more than 5 percent slump, with a batch of stock market debuts and concerns over the health of its economy looming large.               \par \par \uc2 Bourses across Asia, emerging Europe and the Gulf region fell, with Russian rouble-denominated shares down 0.6 percent and Polish stocks almost matching that fall.               \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Turkish stocks extended Friday's gains, adding 0.3 percent and shrugging off Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's move to impose a raft of economic sanctions on Ankara.               \par \par \uc2 "That move was anticipated by markets, Russia was expected to follow through on it," said Cristian Maggio, head of emerging markets strategy at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 , adding the effect of the sanctions on Turkish assets would materialise slowly but steadily, with the lira vulnerable to more weakness.               \par \par \uc2 "It is something that is still developing - there is a lot at stake. In this context, Turkey has more to lose from this situation than Russia has."               \par \par \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  said Turkish tourism would lose out but calculated the maximum losses to the economy at around 0.5 percent of annual gross domestic product.               \par \par \uc2 The lira strengthened 0.4 percent against the dollar after briefly hitting the weakest level in a month early in the session. Turkish dollar-denominated bonds extended losses, however, with most trading lower for the sixth straight session.               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yuan jumped in offshore trade on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Beijing just hours before the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's decision that is expected to grant the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  global reserve status.               \par \par \uc2 "(It) is almost a foregone conclusion, but there is still uncertainty about what weight it will be given," wrote SEB's Per Hammarlund. "A short term bounce in the CNY is possible, but the weakening trend will soon resume with China moving to ease monetary policy and the U.S. tightening."               \par \par \uc2 Other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  painted a more mixed picture, with Russia's rouble trading 0.1 percent weaker against the greenback while South Africa's rand chalked up the same amount in gains, though both were on track to slip 4 percent on the month.               \par \par \uc2 In central and eastern Europe, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  nudged lower against the euro, with Poland's zloty unchanged as data showed the economy expanded in the third quarter of 2015 by 3.5 percent thanks to strong domestic demand.               \par \par \uc2 Across emerging markets, investors were still broadly in wait-and-see mode, with some major \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decisions coming up. While there are growing expectations that the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could cut could cut interest rates by 20 basis points on Thursday, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is widely tipped to hike U.S. interest rates at its mid-December policy meeting.               \par \par \uc2 "We are going into the most uncertain month of the year, and probably of the past several years," said Maggio.                              \par \par \uc2 For GRAPHIC on emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  performance 2015, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/jus35t"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/jus35t}}}\par \par \uc2 For GRAPHIC on \uc2 MSCI\uc2  emerging index performance 2015, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/weh36s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/weh36s}}}\par \par \uc2 For GRAPHIC on \uc2 MSCI\uc2  emerging Europe performance 2015, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/jun28s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/jun28s}}}\par \par \uc2 For GRAPHIC on \uc2 MSCI\uc2  frontier index performance 2015, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/zyh97s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/zyh97s}}}\par \par \uc2 For CENTRAL EUROPE market report, see               \par \par \uc2 For TURKISH market report, see               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-11-30T12:14:11.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020151130ebbu00cqw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
