\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Yuan to see worst month since Aug devaluation ahead of SDR decision\b0\par\par\uc2 921 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2015\par 07:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Onshore yuan at 3-month low; \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint 3-month low\par     * Offshore yuan jumps on suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     * Rupee leads November Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slides on capital outflows\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Nov 30 (Reuters) - China's yuan was set on Monday\par to suffer its largest monthly loss since August, when it was\par devalued by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as markets wait to hear whether\par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  will the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in its global\par reserve basket.\par     Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slumped on a strong dollar\par and weak equity markets, especially in China. Regional\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were also on course for monthly declines as \par expectations that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  will raise interest\par rates next month gain traction.\par      The yuan earlier hit a three-month low of 6.3980\par per dollar but erased most of its losses in volatile trade.\par      \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the daily guidance rate\par  at 6.3962, the weakest since August. \par     The renminbi has slumped 1.2 percent against the dollar \par this month, which would be the largest monthly depreciation\par since a 2.6 percent fall in August, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thomson Reuters\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par data.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  is widely expected to include the yuan in its\par Special Drawing Rights reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket. The move is seen\par as supporting the yuan in the longer term as central banks\par gradually add yuan assets to their reserves.\par     Still, the move is unlikely to bolster the renminbi\par immediately, analysts said.\par     "The market will be looking out for details of the\par announcement, including any requirement for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to refrain\par from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 OCBC Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a\par note.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  took various steps including market\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb the yuan's weakness, especially after the\par yuan devaluation on Aug. 11. On Monday, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  jumped in\par offshore trade on suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par     Some analysts expected Beijing to allow further weakness in\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after it is formally included, given China's \par slowing economy. That will drag on other emerging Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , they added.\par     "Should the authorities allow the yuan to be more market\par determined following SDR inclusion, this could see further\par weakness given that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  has been easing policy while the\par U.S. Fed is set to hike interest rates at their next meeting,"\par said Khoon Goh, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist for ANZ in Singapore.\par     "A weaker yuan will have some spillover effects on Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\par         \par     STORMY NOVEMBER\par     Emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were poised to report monthly\par losses in November on expectations of a U.S. liftoff at its next\par policy meeting in mid-December.\par     India's rupee led regional depreciation with a 2.3\par percent slide against the dollar this month, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thomson Reuters\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par data showed.\par     Foreign investors dumped Indian stocks and bonds after Prime\par Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Narendra Modi\f28 \uc2 \f2 's BJP suffered a heavy defeat in Bihar\par state's elections, raising concerns the government would\par struggle to pass policy reforms.\par     The South Korean won slumped 1.5 percent this\par month as foreign investors sold out of Seoul's stock market\par .\par     Indonesia's rupiah has fallen 1.2 percent so far\par November on corporate dollar demand for year-end payments. The\par Singapore dollar has lost 0.8 percent on the yuan's\par weakness.\par     The Thai baht has slipped 0.9 percent, while the\par Philippine peso ended the month down 0.7 percent. Philippine\par financial markets were closed for a holiday on Monday.\par     Investors are keeping an eye on Fed Chair \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2 's\par speech on Wednesday and October U.S. jobs data on Friday for any\par clue on U.S. monetary policy. \par     In contrast, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to add\par monetary stimulus at its Thursday meeting. That may limit\par downside in emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Malaysia's ringgit bucked the regional depreciation\par trend in November with a 0.8 percent gain.\par     The ringgit rose as a rebound in crude oil prices eased some\par concerns over Malaysia's falling oil and gas revenues.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also drew support from an agreement by the\par debt-ridden state fund \f28 \uc2 \f2 1Malaysia Development Bhd\f28 \uc2 \f2  to sell its\par energy business to \f28 \uc2 \f2 China General Nuclear Power Corporation\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a\par $2.3 billion cash deal. China will also buy more Malaysian\par government bonds, Premier \f28 \uc2 \f2 Li Keqiang\f28 \uc2 \f2  was quoted as saying.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0520 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       122.70         122.75       +0.04\par   Sing dlr        1.4126         1.4125       -0.01\par   Taiwan dlr      32.681         32.815       +0.41\par   Korean won     1157.30        1153.00       -0.37\par   Baht             35.86          35.85       -0.03\par   Peso             47.15          47.15       +0.00\par   Rupiah        13835.00       13775.00       -0.43\par   Rupee            66.81          66.76       -0.07\par   Ringgit         4.2590         4.2600       +0.02\par   Yuan            6.3967         6.3952       -0.02\par  \par   Change so far in 2015\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       122.70         119.66       -2.48\par   Sing dlr        1.4126         1.3260       -6.13\par   Taiwan dlr      32.681         31.718       -2.95\par   Korean won     1157.30        1099.30       -5.01\par   Baht             35.86          32.90       -8.25\par   Peso             47.15          44.72       -5.14\par   Rupiah        13835.00       12380.00      -10.52\pa