\par\par \b \uc2 Offshore Yuan up on Likely  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Before IMF Decision -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 188 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2015\par 07:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0613 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Blink and you may have missed it. The offshore yuan was trading at a more than two-month low against the U.S. dollar early Monday before it abruptly spiked. USD/CNH was at 6.4613, then collapsed to 6.4290 implying a stronger yuan, before bouncing back to 6.4460 and then dropping back to 6.4230. A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader based in Hong Kong, where the offshore yuan market is based, said the market suspects \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen the yuan. The boost comes just before the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  is expected to announce the outcome of its five-year review of the composition of its Special Drawing Rights \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket. The \uc2 IMF\uc2 , which meets at its headquarters in Washington later today, has hinted that the yuan will be included in the SDR, conferring it the status of global reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/CNH is now 6.4302 from its Friday close of 6.4506. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Editor: KLH\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 30-11-15 0639GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020151130ebbu00074\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
