\par\par \b \uc2 IMF\uc2 's yuan inclusion signals less risk taking in China\b0\par\par\uc2 1,090 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2015\par 02:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * \uc2 IMF\uc2  expected to agree on Monday to add yuan to \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves               \par \par \uc2 * Raises optimism inclusion will spur fresh capital account reform               \par \par \uc2 * Resistance to reform could grow though, Chinese sources say               \par \par \uc2 * \uc2 IMF\uc2  inclusion to boost yuan demand by $600 bln plus - analysts               \par \par \uc2 By Pete Sweeney and Krista Hughes               \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI/NEW YORK, Nov 30 (Reuters) - When the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  agrees on Monday to add the Chinese yuan to its reserves basket in the biggest shake-up in more than three decades, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  can afford itself a congratulatory nod.               \par \par \uc2 By acknowledging the yuan as a major global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  alongside the dollar, euro, yen, and pound, as is widely expected, \uc2 IMF\uc2  members will endorse the efforts of China's economic reformers and by doing so hope that will spur fresh change in China.               \par \par \uc2 But Chinese policy insiders and international policymakers say reforms may not continue at the breakneck pace of recent months. In addition, Chinese sources suggest adding the yuan to the \uc2 IMF\uc2  basket leaves economic conservatives better positioned to resist further significant reform in a reminder of the period following China's entry to the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2  (\uc2 WTO\uc2 ).               \par \par \uc2 A slowing in the pace has implications for those who bet that making the yuan a global reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will give it a boost. The yuan has fallen almost 3 percent against the dollar this year, on course for its biggest annual fall since its landmark 2005 revaluation.               \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  decision will remove a key incentive - bolstering national pride - that reformers used to push otherwise reluctant conservatives to support reforms.               \par \par \uc2 More importantly, however, are worries in Beijing that the rickety economy can't handle more aggressive reform that allows a freer flow of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  across China's borders.               \par \par \uc2 Beijing is already rapidly losing a taste for more experimentation with capital flows, say the sources - economists involved in policy discussions who declined to be identified because of the sensitivity of the subject.               \par \par \uc2 After the stock market buckled more than 40 percent in the summer - which many blamed on nefarious foreign capital - regulators have made it harder for money to leave \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to counter yuan selling pressure and have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in onshore and offshore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Not just conservatives, but  more liberal economists are calling for a pause.               \par \par \uc2 "Our ability to control financial risk has yet to be improved," said a senior economist at the China Centre for International Economic Exchanges (CCIEE), an influential Beijing think-tank.               \par \par \uc2 "Any rush to open up the capital account completely could be unfavourable for controlling financial risks ... we will definitely be very cautious."               \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2 's executive board, representing the Fund's 188 members, is likely to approve inclusion of the yuan in the reserve basket, known as Special Drawing Rights (SDR). An \uc2 IMF\uc2  staff report and Managing Director \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  have endorsed the idea. The United States has suggested it will not stand in the way.               \par \par \uc2 The SDR basket determines the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mix countries like Greece receive when the \uc2 IMF\uc2  disburses financial aid. Some economists predict inclusion will boost demand for the yuan, or renminbi (RMB), by more than $600 billion.               \par \par \uc2 Chinese media predicted entry will draw over 1 trillion yuan ($156 billion) of foreign money into China bonds. Both predictions rest on assumptions more capital account opening is on the way.               \par \par \uc2 "The RMB (will be) included so the reform-oriented forces can keep the upper hand; there's no way back now for the conservative members in the party," said an \uc2 IMF\uc2  policymaker from an advanced economy, who spoke on condition of anonymity.               \par \par \uc2 Seeing the SDR decision as the goal of China's reforms is "as if the tail were wagging the dog," said Otaviano Canuto, who represents Brazil and other Latin American and Caribbean countries on the \uc2 IMF\uc2  board.               \par \par \uc2 "The expectation is that the eventual inclusion of the RMB is a moment in the process ... (which) is being undertaken and implemented for itself, because it's part of the development of the Chinese economy," he said.                              \par \par \uc2 "WHY WOULD YOU TAKE MORE RISKS?"               \par \par \uc2 China has pushed to make the yuan more international, setting up swap arrangements with countries so trade can be settled in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and China has said it will push ahead with financial reform. It has widened the yuan's trading band and this year went a long way to freeing up interest rates.               \par \par \uc2 But Chinese policy advisers have always been divided, sometimes publicly, on how far China should go in opening up its borders to foreign capital; while few use vocabulary that rejects general reform principles, many domestic policy advisors - including some otherwise supportive of economic liberalisation - warn throwing open the gates to cross-border flows would be destabilising.               \par \par \uc2 They have many quiet allies among China's state-owned banks and other inefficient industries, which fear that a freer market for capital will expose them to international competition and put them out of business.               \par \par \uc2 Foreign access to financial markets is still tightly restricted and of late regulators have reversed some measures that were designed to make it easier to move the yuan offshore.               \par \par \uc2 "The (Chinese) reform camp has been selling (\uc2 IMF\uc2  inclusion) partly on the basis of international prestige, in particular equalling Japan," said Derek Scissors, chief economist at China Beige Book.               \par \par \uc2 "What is the reform movement going to say now to move reform forward ... if the \uc2 IMF\uc2  has already recognised China as an internationalised \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?" asked Scissors. "Why would you take more risks?"               \par \par \uc2 Some see parallels with China's \uc2 WTO\uc2  entry in 2001, in which Chinese reformers used entry negotiations as an incentive to push through painful state sector restructuring, only to see their agenda sidelined shortly after inclusion.               \par \par \uc2 China's retreat from its \uc2 WTO\uc2  commitments was widely blamed on the retirement in 2003 of reform-minded Premier Zhu Rongji. Current leading reformer \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2 , the head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  who has said the yuan will be basically convertible by this year, is at 67 years old already past the typical retirement age for senior Communist Party officials.               \par \par \uc2 "Previous wording was 'accelerating' convertibility, now it's 'making RMB convertible in an orderly manner,'" said an economist, of the China Academy of Social Sciences (CASS), who is also close to policy discussions. He said China was well aware capital account liberalisation elsewhere - in particular Japan - has been blamed for causing "serious crises."               \par \par \uc2 "The most important thing is to handle domestic issues well; we cannot afford to see another collapse of the stock market."               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-11-30T02:23:18.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020151130ebbu001uv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
