\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Chinese companies are betting on further yuan weakness\b0\par\par\uc2 944 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2015\par 07:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Craig Stephen, \uc2 MarketWatch\par \par \uc2 Record-breaking purchases in Hong Kong make sense if they are hedges against a falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (\uc2 MarketWatch\uc2 ) -- In recent weeks it has appeared the worst is over for the yuan, which has been under pressure since its surprise August devaluation.\par \par \uc2 October data showed China had reversed ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/chinas-foreign-exchange-reserves-rise-11-39-billion-in-october-1446870527?alg=y)five"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.wsj.com/articles/chinas-\b  \highlight22foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight22 -reserves-rise-11-39-billion-in-october-1446870527?alg=y)five}}}\uc2  months of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  outflows, while there was also better news on China's hopes for the yuan's inclusion by the \uc2 IMF\uc2  as one its special drawing right reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , helping it on its way to being a reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This looks increasingly likely after positive comments ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.marketwatch.com/story/imfs-lagarde-and-staff-support-adding-chinas-currency-to-sdr-basket-2015-11-13"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.marketwatch.com/story/imfs-lagarde-and-staff-support-adding-chinas-\b  \highlight22currency\b0 \highlight22 -to-sdr-basket-2015-11-13}}}\uc2 ) by IMF Managing Director \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Yet there remain various question marks over whether the yuan has really turned a corner after its summer wobbles. There is scant evidence the economy has, and much more of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including in offshore markets.\par \par \uc2 Another way to gauge the market's view on the yuan is to ignore the government and watch what China's corporations are doing with their money. Increasingly it seems they are going long overseas assets, while loading up on yuan debt at home -- a trade that would be advantageous if there were further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness.\par \par \uc2 As China's de facto offshore financial center, Hong Kong needs to be watched.\par \par \uc2 Its commercial property market has witnessed a remarkable influx of buying by mainland Chinese corporates. Last week there was a record-breaking purchase ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mingtiandi.com/real-estate/outbound-investment/evergrande-buys-hk-office-tower-from-chinese-estates-for-1-61b/)by"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mingtiandi.com/real-estate/outbound-investment/evergrande-buys-hk-office-tower-from-chinese-estates-for-1-61b/)by}}}\uc2  Guangzhou-based property giant China Evergrande when it paid HK$12.5 billion for an office tower in Wanchai district.\par \par \uc2 Despite not being in prime Central, this set a new mark for the highest price paid per square foot in Hong Kong at HK$36,232. Earlier this year, \uc2 Hopewell Holdings\uc2  secured $35,000 for sale of a floor in 9 Queen's Road.\par \par \uc2 On the same day, China Life announced it had purchased an en bloc office tower with a two-story retail block for HK$5.85 billion from local conglomerate Wheelock.\par \par \uc2 Analysts have questioned the steep price of the Evergrande purchase in particular. Yet these investments would look different if there were an expectation of yuan depreciation. Due to the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the greenback , such assets offer a natural hedge.\par \par \uc2 If you rewind all the way to 1994 when China last had a significant yuan depreciation, there was an influx of money from over the border seeking similar protection.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, another sign that Chinese corporations are positioning for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  realignment is in the growth in onshore debt raising at the same time wider foreign acquisitions are skyrocketing.\par \par \uc2 According to Dealogic, year to date China's onshore corporate debt capital market volume has reached 312.8 billion yuan ($50.2 billion), almost four times levels from a year earlier. The financing is also cheaper than overseas. Earlier this year, Evergrande raised 40 billion yuan in the domestic bond market, at less than half the cost it paid for an offshore issuance of US$1 billion in February.\par \par \uc2 This comes at the same time Chinese corporations are also ramping up acquisitions of overseas companies. So far this year Chinese acquirers have announced a total of $486.1 billion in mergers and acquisitions, up more than 50% on the same period last year, according to Dealogic. This has been given a push by authorities, who in May relaxed rules so that any Chinese firms looking to invest less than US$1billion in overseas firms no longer needed to seek government approval.\par \par \uc2 Other signs of ongoing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pressure can be seen in Hong Kong, the main offshore yuan hub.\par \par \uc2 Since the initial yuan devaluation there has been a waning appetite for yuan, which fell some 84 billion ($13 billion) in September from August to 895 billion yuan, according to \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) data. Meanwhile the HKMA had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times last month to stop the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rising, buying more than $10 billion, the highest monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 2009. At the same time this pushed the Hong Kong interbank offered rate (HIBOR) down to record low levels.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile analysts remain cautious about the prospects for the yuan, despite the likely positive news on SDR inclusion. Societe General writes in a new report that, although there is likely to be modest short-term positive reaction, inclusion by itself is unlikely to drive the trend.\par \par \uc2 They estimate we may see capital flows relating to SDR allocation of just $30 billion and $40 billion depending on the weights. Furthermore, this inclusion does not mean that reserve managers will have to mechanically buy more Chinese assets now or in the future.\par \par \uc2 UBS\uc2  is similarly sanguine on \uc2 IMF\uc2  endorsement providing much of a boost to the yuan as reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They write in a new report that more needs to be done by Chinese authorities to open up China's capital accounts and convince global reserve managers to invest meaningfully in Chinese reserve-related assets.\par \par \uc2 For now when it comes to having a view on the yuan, it looks best to follow what Chinese corporations are doing, rather than what their government is saying.\par \par \uc2 -Craig Stephen; 415-439-6400; AskNewswires@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 19-11-15 0655GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020151119ebbj0005t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
