\par\par \b \uc2 Beijing Softens Yuan Management\b0\par\par\uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2015\par 08:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par C2\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL 11/13/15) \par    By Anjani Trivedi \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's typically ham-handed approach to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  -- exemplified by its market-wrenching August devaluation -- may be giving way to a lighter touch.\f28 \par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, traders say, Beijing has been getting its message out more subtly, buying and selling in the freely traded offshore yuan and forwards markets.\par \par \uc2 They praise the change, saying it boosts their confidence in China's efforts to guide the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and calm markets.\par \par \uc2 "We understand more about their reaction function and thought process," said Beng-Hong Lee, head of markets for China at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The change, though, also highlights the shortcomings of a largely closed-off capital account that leads to different values for yuan trading inside and outside the country. And it risks turning those offshore markets, now freely traded, into ones heavily influenced by China.\par \par \uc2 Beijing's shift is largely the product of its pursuit of reserve-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  status for the yuan, alongside the U.S. dollar, yen, sterling and euro. Even as the country's wider financial overhaul has slowed down in recent months, Beijing has rapidly ticked off the goals set for the yuan by the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , which is expected to make a decision on including the yuan in its reserve-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket in coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 One hurdle -- that countries be able to freely buy and sell the yuan -- could prove difficult even with this new style of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The shift does put the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  more in line with other emerging-market central banks, which routinely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to damp volatility or control the value of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is still trying to win back credibility that was lost when its summer devaluation caught investors off guard and sent a ripple of anxiety through global markets. Within days, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in full volatility-quelling mode, holding a rare news conference and injecting billions of dollars into the financial system.\par \par \uc2 "Market confidence is very critical to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Charles Feng, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  in Hong Kong, especially as China gradually opens its capital account. China has largely defused market fears of rapid depreciation, he said: "Recent action reflects that priority."\par \par \uc2 Beyond the symbolism, Beijing has good reason to want reserve-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  status. \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  estimates that global central banks will purchase $85 billion to $125 billion of yuan next year if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is included in the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's basket and $56 billion if it isn't.\par \par \uc2 Until last month, China had suffered several months of falling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, though it still has a war chest of $3.5 trillion. That could be one reason the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  started to use the forwards market, analysts say. Its buying of yuan there doesn't show up in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves immediately, unlike buying in the spot market.\par \par \uc2 One of the biggest challenges for Beijing is the yuan's two exchange rates. In recent months, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has worked to bring the two rates within a consistent, narrower range.\par \par \uc2 For years, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's heavy-handed \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  succeeded in quickly moving the market where Beijing wanted it to be, but the lack of warning or guidance -- which contrasted with the practices of other central banks -- frustrated investors.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were often followed by a tug of war between the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the market, with the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  ultimately quashing the pressure by selling U.S. dollars through the country's large state banks, as in the days after the devaluation.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 13, 2015 02:44 ET (07:44 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020151113ebbd000rs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
