\par\par \b \uc2 Is the Fed Made in China, Too? -- Barron's Asia\b0\par\par\uc2 By William Pesek   \par \uc2 873 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2015\par 01:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 China, it's often said, is the world's factory floor. But is the same now true of central banking?\par \par \uc2 The last 20 years were about the internationalization of \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  policies. Globalization vastly expanded the Fed's reach from 12 U.S. districts to de facto ones in Latin America, Russia, Southeast Asia and, of course, China. But as \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  tries to pull off the Fed's first rate hike in a decade, she confronts a monetary landscape brimming with Chinese characteristics.\par \par \uc2 This riddle fascinates monetary gurus like Eswar Prasad and Barry Eichengreen. When I caught up with Prasad in Seoul recently, the Cornell University economist was dubious about China becoming a "safe haven" anytime soon. Markets, after all, are bidding the yuan lower amid fears about China's trajectory. For all the platitudes about putting a 6.5% floor under growth, punters are clearly betting that the August devaluation won't be Beijing's last.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  is tacitly confirming the negativity by drawing down reserves. It's selling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dollars in particular, to keep the yuan from crashing. Odd considering the rhetoric in Washington about Beijing manipulating the yuan and killing U.S. jobs. The reality is that wealthy Chinese are scrambling to spirit their millions -- and billions -- overseas. This downward pressure has the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  selling roughly $60 billion of U.S. Treasuries per month to support the yuan -- efforts likely to accelerate going forward.\par \par \uc2 Connecting the dots in a Project Syndicate op-ed, Eichengreen of \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\uc2 , wonders if China isn't already doing the Fed's tightening job. Its third round of quantitative easing in 2012, remember, upped monthly bond purchases by $40 billion -- and eventually to around $85 billion. China's $60 billion dollar-sales figure is dead in the middle of that range, meaning it's well ahead of the Fed in draining liquidity. Call it "outsourced monetary tightening," as do James Hamilton and Menzie Chinn in their Econbrower blog.\par \par \uc2 There are many conclusions to draw here. One is that those who worry Fed Chair Yellen is behind the tightening curve can relax a bit. Another is that the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  may be under even more pressure to add fresh liquidity to markets. The most important may be how the uniquely symbiotic relationship between the Fed and \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is altering monetary science.\par \par \uc2 It's been 10 years since \uc2 C. Fred Bergsten\uc2  popularized the idea that the \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  would eventually be eclipsed by the Group of Two. In 2006, the G-2 manifested itself in President George W. Bush's White House initiating the annual China-U.S. Strategic Economic Dialogue. But the last 12 months demonstrate how difficult it is to discern where America's economy ends and China's begins. That's long been the case on trade. China relies on U.S. households consuming just as much as the U.S. relies on the banker in Beijing. But less attention has been paid to how the Fed and the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  are engaged in a monetary tug of war.\par \par \uc2 This complicates Yellen's thinking. The last thing she wants is to be blamed for crashing Asia's biggest economy. Or Hong Kong, which is buckling under a strong dollar thanks to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg. Other emerging economies are trapped in the middle of a Fed and \uc2 PBOC\uc2  moving in opposite directions, especially commodity-producing ones. High corporate debt levels across Asia are another concern. The Fed's tightening cycle that began in 1994 helped precipitate Asia's 1997 crisis as dollar loans become impossible to repay.\par \par \uc2 In Seoul, Prasad, author of the "The Dollar Trap," explained that the yuan has the potential to become a key \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within 10 years. That requires a whole range of bold reforms to the nation's financial, legal and industrial policies. The dollar, he argues, will "continue to be dominant for the time being."\par \par \uc2 YET CHINA IS ALTERING THE MECHANICS OF FED POLICY. Given the magnitude of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months -- perhaps in the $100 billion range since Beijing's devaluation -- President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  has already tightened for Yellen to the tune of about 25 basis points. "The problem is that no one knows how long capital outflows from China will persist or how long the Chinese authorities will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Eichengreen argues. "From this standpoint, the Fed's decision to wait to begin liftoff is eminently sensible."\par \par \uc2 As this column has argued before, the Fed will get away with fewer rate hikes than economists assume. Fifteen years into its own QE odyssey, Japan is still easing policy to placate a financial system addicted to free money. The Fed may fare better in normalizing rates. But Beijing's monetary maneuvers are seeping into Washington's in unpredictable ways. Like just about everything else in the U.S. these days, Fed policy is being made in China.\par \par \uc2 Comments? E-mail us at william.pesek@barrons.com\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Comments? E-mail us at asiaeditors@barrons.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 11, 2015 19:36 ET (00:36 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020151112ebbc0003j\par }\page 