\par\par \uc2 Markets Main\par \b \uc2 China's  \highlight22\uc2 Foreign-Exchange \highlight\uc2  Reserves Rise, Snapping Streak of Outflows\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chuin-Wei Yap   \par \uc2 773 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2015\par 01:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top North American Equities Stories\par \uc2 DJTNAE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI\u8212\'20\'14China's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  hoard rose in October, snapping a five-month streak of capital outflows in a development that could help Beijing shore up the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The rise reported by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Saturday follows five consecutive monthly declines. It indicates weakening investor expectations of a depreciating yuan and Beijing's successes in stemming the outflow of illegal money transfers out of the country.\par \par \uc2 Still, as worries remain over {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/china-economic-growth-falls-below-7-for-first-time-since-2009-1445221368"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 slowing growth  }}}\uc2  in the world's second-largest economy, it is likely that \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue its monetary-easing policies to shore up growth. Last month, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/chinas-central-bank-cuts-rates-1445601495"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 China lowered interest rates  }}}\uc2 for the sixth time over the past year.\par \par \uc2 China's reserve levels rose by $11.39 billion to $3.526 trillion in October from a month earlier, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  said. This is only the third time China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have risen in the last 15 months and the first since dipping sharply in August after China's surprise move to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/china-moves-to-devalue-the-yuan-1439258401"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 devalue its \b  \highlight22currency\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Changes in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are a proxy for capital inflows and outflows, though money also moves past China's borders {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/in-chinas-alleyways-underground-banks-move-money-1445911877"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in ways officials can't track  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Outflows are a relatively new puzzle for Beijing. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves piled up for more than a decade as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars flowing into its money supply from the country's massive export volumes. As the world's No. 2 economy slows, the direction of its capital movement has reversed.\par \par \uc2 Outflows reached a historic volume after \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  devalued the yuan in mid-August, then was forced to step up its selling of dollar assets, particularly U.S. Treasurys, to counter a sharp selloff in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The move led to China's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/china-august-forex-reserves-down-by-93-9-billion-as-pboc-intervenes-1441614856"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight22  reserves falling by a record  }}}\uc2  monthly $93.9 billion in August, its steepest percentage drop since March 2012.\par \par \uc2 Those pressures have eased as \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  signal it is unlikely the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will tolerate further major movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at least to the end of the year, analysts say. The summer's slump in Chinese stock markets {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/japan-shares-rise-on-yen-weakness-1446688340"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has also reversed  }}}\uc2 , potentially giving investors more reason to keep their money inside the country.\par \par \uc2 "We think these outflows are now reversing because market expectations for significant renminbi depreciation have now eased after \uc2 PBOC\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and expectations for U.S. rate hikes have been pushed back," Capital Economics economist Julian Evans-Pritchard said, referring to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by its other name.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  's decision not to raise rates at its September meeting made capital less likely to flee China for the U.S. seeking higher returns. The Fed last week kept alive the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/feds-yellen-december-is-live-possibility-for-first-rate-increase-1446654282"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 possibility of a rate increase  }}}\uc2  in December. Such a renewed likelihood could again amp up outflow pressures on China in coming weeks, though that is likely to be outweighed by expectations that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  would continue to put a floor under yuan depreciation.\par \par \uc2 "We have long held the view that the capital outflows pressure is manageable," said Larry Hu, China economist for Macquarie Securities.\par \par \uc2 Illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transfers out of the country were a factor in the decline, and reducing such flows are likely a factor in the reversal, analysts say. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are released back to the market when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells its dollar holdings\u8212\'20\'14in a reversal of what it does with foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows during a boom\u8212\'20\'14to support the yuan, but not all of it makes it out of the country via approved investments. Some end up spirited offshore in amounts beyond China's legal limits in search of hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it is escalating a campaign to stem illicit outflows, which are often associated with money laundering.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has estimated that outflows attributed to illegal underground banks amounted to about 800 billion yuan ($125 billion) from April to October this year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to spend to prevent its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from sharply falling further. The onshore yuan on Oct. 30 posted its biggest one-day gain since March 2014 after an apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders said authorities could be boosting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  's meeting later this month, when it will determine the composition of its elite basket of reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Rose Yu contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Chuin-Wei Yap at \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTNAE1120151109ebb900010\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
