\par\par \b \uc2 China's Yuan Weakens Sharply; Japan leads Asian Shares Lower--Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chao Deng   \par \uc2 901 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2015\par 11:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The latest signs that China is struggling to turn its economy around in the fourth quarter sent most Asian markets lower and the onshore yuan sharply weaker on Monday.\par \par \uc2 In late afternoon trade, the yuan fell as much as 0.46% to 6.3464, marking its biggest one-day depreciation since the days after Chinese officials devalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August. The onshore yuan last traded at 6.3379, compared with 6.3175 at its close on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell even as \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 6.3154 against the U.S. dollar earlier, 0.54% stronger than Friday's daily reference rate, which represented the largest percentage change since July 2005.\par \par \uc2 The onshore yuan can trade 2% above or below the daily fixing level.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Japan shares fell from a two-month high, leading most of the region lower, as a rally fueled by stimulus hopes there fizzled.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei Stock Average fell 2.1%, led by sectors seen as bellwethers of the domestic economy: utilities, financial firms and consumer-discretionary companies. The losses signal disappointment after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held back from additional stimulus measures on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Australia's S&P/ASX 200 lost 1.4%, Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index fell 1.2% and the Shanghai Composite Index shed 1.7%.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's Kospi, however, gained 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, a private gauge of China's manufacturing activity rose to 48.3 in October, marking the eighth-straight month of contraction. The reading, which tends to capture more private enterprises than the official gauge, came in at 47.2 in September.\par \par \uc2 A reading above 50 indicates expansion in activity, while one below that level signals contraction.\par \par \uc2 The reading comes on the heels of China's official gauge of factory activity in October, released Sunday, which contracted unexpectedly.\par \par \uc2 "There's some fairly big pessimism that the economy will continue slowing in the fourth quarter," said Evan Lucas, a market analyst at brokerage IG.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lucas added that the stronger yuan fixing likely was a reaction to Sunday's data, but also a signal of Chinese authorities' intentions to moderate the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline since the devaluation.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the onshore yuan had posted its biggest one-day gain since March 2014 amid apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders said authorities could be boosting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's meeting later this month, when it will determine the composition of its elite basket of reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . China has been vying for the yuan's inclusion as the country seeks to raise its status on the global stage.\par \par \uc2 Traders said many are awaiting U.S. economic data later this week and didn't see signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic readings are yet another signal of its stalling economy, which has unsettled markets. On Sunday, China's Premier \uc2 Li Keqiang\uc2  said the country will expand at a pace of "at least" 6.5% annually over the next five years, marking the first time China's leaders publicly have given a target for annual growth lower than 7% in coming years.\par \par \uc2 Over the past several weeks, worries about slowing growth in China and elsewhere fueled investors' bets on further stimulus from global central banks. That helped shares in the region close out October with their best monthly performance since May 2009.\par \par \uc2 But the rally is quickly fading.\par \par \uc2 Investors say they are worried that Asia still faces headwinds from lower commodity prices and a higher U.S. dollar, which makes it more expensive to pay off dollar-denominated debts.\par \par \uc2 "The recovery globally is starting to show some signs of fatigue," said Matthew Sherwood, head of investment strategy at Perpetual Investment Ltd. "Markets are oscillating between up and down [days] more frequently."\par \par \uc2 In Japan, shares of \uc2 Sharp Corp\uc2 . tumbled 4.5% to Yen127 yen a share, a new low for the year, after the display maker on Friday logged a six-month operating loss and said it is talks with various firms, which could lead to a cash injection or a selloff of the firm's display unit.\par \par \uc2 In China, shares of semiconductor manufacturer Ningbo Kangqiang Electronics Co. and real-estate firm \uc2 Deluxe Family Co\uc2 . both fell by the maximum 10% allowed by regulators on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Both stocks had been bought up by Shanghai-based Zexi Investment, whose manager, Xu Xiang, has been arrested for alleged insider trading and stock-price manipulation, according to the official \uc2 Xinhua News Agency\uc2  Sunday.\par \par \uc2 In South Korea, Shares of \uc2 Samsung SDI Co\uc2 . were up 4.2%, as the battery maker is expected to rake in more $2 billion from \uc2 Samsung Group\uc2 's plans to sell off 90% of its chemical business to \uc2 Lotte Chemical\uc2  , as well as its 15% stake in \uc2 Samsung Fine Chemicals\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Brent crude oil was flat at $49.57 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Gold prices were down 0.2% at $1,139.70 a troy ounce.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the Dow Jones Industrial Average and S&P 500 both lost 0.5%, weighed down by financial companies.\par \par \uc2 Anjani Trivedi, Mayumi Negishi and Yifan Xie contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Chao Deng at Chao.Deng@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 02, 2015 05:48 ET (10:48 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020151102ebb2000z2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
