\par\par \b \uc2 China Caixin PMI Up, but HK Traders Note It's Still Negative -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,811 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2015\par 03:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] Hong Kong stocks sold off despite a sharp improvement in the Caixin manufacturing purchasing managers' index--this index came in at 48.3 in October, up from 47.2 the month before. This improvement contrasts with official data reported by China's statistics agency, which said Sunday that the manufacturing sector stayed the same at 49.8 from September to October. Both numbers are below 50, meaning that the sector is still contracting. But "the slight upswing shows the manufacturing industry's overall weakening has slowed down, indicating that previous stimulating measures have begun to take effect," He Fan, chief economist of the Caixin Insight Group, says in a statement. Hong Kong stocks sold off slightly after release of the Caixin data--the Hang Seng Index was down 0.8% pre data, and is now down 1%. (Dominique.fong@wsj.com; Twitter: @dominiquefong) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] China's end to the one-child policy won't stem the population decline, because the number of potential new mothers is falling too, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . Birth numbers have been on a steady decline since the 1980s, which means the number of women that could become mothers is falling too. The bank expects the number of new mothers in China to shrink 35% in the next 15 years. "This implies that the number of births per marriage will need to rise from 1.25 in 2014 to 1.89 by 2030 just to keep annual births flat," it says. (jacky.wong@wsj.com; @jackycwong) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] Even the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  would have been surprised by last week's third-quarter inflation figures. It explains the current heightened uncertainty about whether or not the RBA will respond with a cut in interest rates at a policy meeting Tuesday. Market betting is finely balanced at around 50% for a cut, while around one third of economists expect the first easing since May, taking the cash rate to 1.75% from 2.0%. History is no guide. The RBA has remained sidelined at its November meetings since 2011. Low inflation, concerns about world growth and the prospect of soft GDP growth in 2016 as housing construction slows down argue in favor of a cut now. Other central banks are cutting too. Meanwhile, arguments for no cut include a still-effervescent property market (although there are signs of moderation), solid business conditions, firm employment growth, and stable commodity prices. A sharply lower Australian dollar is helping services exports and the RBA has been upbeat on the economy of late. The RBA might well ask what would another cut achieve outside of running the risk of refueling housing demand while doing little for the broader economy. The problem with Australia's economy is not the cost of funds, even after banks raised mortgage rate last month. Cutting rates at a time when the bank regulator has put curbs on property speculation might also seem counter productive. The RBA will also want to keep some ammunition in reserve to battle fires yet to flame. The case for the RBA to sit on its hands still seems attractive. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter: @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ITS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 JP Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  now expects the BOJ to ease monetary policy in Nov. 2016, as BOJ policy-steering has taken on a "somewhat backward-looking manner," rather than a forward-looking one, the brokerage's analysts say. The BOJ kept its policy on hold at Friday's meeting, while downgrading its growth and price views. "The BOJ appears to believe that the positive feedback loop between a rise in income and spending has been established," they say. "Although we do not totally disagree with the BOJ's view, we believe that the virtuous cycle has just started but remains very fragile, and that the BOJ's strong support is needed." If the BOJ were to ease again, more JGB purchases would likely be a core measure, they add. (megumi.fujikawa@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0204 GMT [Dow Jones] The Hang Seng property subindex is down 0.9% at 30,709 after the city's government announced Friday that potential primary housing supply will reach a record high of 86,000 units in the coming three to four years. About 70% are small to medium sized flats of less than 753 square feet, the government said. \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects developers will underperform the market after in-line performance so far this year. "While the momentum of the office market is likely to slow, we believe the biggest potential mispricing opportunities are in the retail landlords, and Wharf (0004.HK) is our top recommendation in this regard," says the investment bank.(yvonne.lee@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0159 GMT [Dow Jones] Thailand's government is stopping the release of higher-quality rice in its warehouses in order not to affect market prices before the new harvest enters the market soon. Commerce Minister Apiradi Tantraporn says that the focus, for now, will be on selling about 2 million tons of degraded rice to the industrial sector, possibly for ethanol production. Chareon Laothamatas, Chairman of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thai Rice Exporters Association\f28 \uc2 \f2 , believes that such an initiative won't affect market rice prices even if this supply will be priced at 5,000 baht ($140.6) per ton. Chareon is confident Thailand's exports of rice will total at least 9.5 million metric tons in 2015 and no less than 10 million metric tons in 2016. "Despite the drought problem across the world, both existing and upcoming supply of [Thai] rice will be sufficient to supply the [world] market," says Chareon. (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com; Twitter: @nopbkk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  benchmarked the yuan 0.5% stronger against the U.S. dollar Monday compared to its last daily fixing rate, but in the spot market the yuan has slipped 0.1% compared to its Friday close of 6.3175 - which was a nearly 3-month peak versus the greenback. The daily USD/CNY fixing rate was moved lower in line with the relatively steep 0.6% appreciation of the offshore-traded yuan (USD/CNH) on Friday - which was due to suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the PBOC, says an offshore trader. With this delayed reaction, the significance of the daily USD/CNY benchmark rate may be diminishing, which could be exactly what China wants to achieve, as it paves the way for the yuan to become a more market-oriented \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . This would enhance the probability of it becoming included in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 's basket of reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sooner. Spot USD/CNY is now 6.3246 from its Friday close of 6.3175. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0145 GMT [Dow Jones] While the latest report shows local economic activity has picked up markedly, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thailand's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains worried about the pressure from external factors on the country's growth this year. Roong Poshyananda Mallikamas, a senior director at the Bank of Thailand, says the Thai economy in 3Q15 has done much better than in the preceding quarter as such key indicators as private consumption and investment saw an improvement alongside stronger business sentiment and continued state spending. "Nevertheless, concerns over the global economic [slowdown] remain, especially the situation in China that has continued to affect the Asean region", notes Roong. "That has worried \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  more." (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com; Twitter: @nopbkk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippines' peso is up 0.2% versus the U.S. dollar Monday, but chart signals suggest that the rally may not last long. The peso, like most other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , is slightly stronger due to another overnight dip in the U.S. dollar index, and also partly due to a notable boost in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yuan - suspected to be due to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The daily USD/PHP chart however may retain a bullish technical stance if the pair ends the day above 46.68. This would keep USD/PHP above the Ichimoku Cloud support zone and also inside the daily Bollinger uptrend channel. Reaffirmation of this chart signals could keep USD/PHP on the path toward the round-figure trading barrier of 47.00. USD/PHP is now 46.77 from its Friday close of 46.82. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0132 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY Intraday: Under pressure. The pair is trading below the 20-period intraday moving average (MA), which is below the 50-period one. The intraday relative strength index (RSI) is badly directed within the area between 50 and 30 while being capped by a declining trend line. The intraday outlook remains bearish. As long as 120.80 holds as the key resistance, the pair is expected to fall toward the first downside target at 120.15 (last seen on October 28) and the second one at 119.95 (last seen on October 22). Trading Central (tradingcentral.com) is a commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (jasper.moiseiwitsch@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0131 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD Intraday: Upside prevails. The pair remains on the upside and is around the 20-period intraday MA, which is above the 50-period one. Meanwhile the intraday RSI is well directed above the neutrality level at 50 calling for a new upleg. As long as 1.0965 holds as the key support, the pair could challenge the first upside target at 1.1070 (around the high of October 30). The second upside target is set at 1.1100 (around the high of October 28). Alternatively, below 1.0965, look for a decline toward 1.0935. Trading Central (tradingcentral.com) is a commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (jasper.moiseiwitsch@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1 Nov 2015 21:27 ET \par China Caixin PMI Up, but HK Traders Note It's -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD Intraday: Upside prevails. The pair remains on the upside and is around the 20-period intraday MA, which is above the 50-period one. And the intraday RSI is well directed above the neutrality level at 50 lacking downward momentum. The pair is approaching the first upside target at 0.7160 (around the high of October 28), the breaking of which would call for a further bounce toward 0.7200. Alternatively, below the key support at 0.7065, look for a decline toward 0.7015 (last seen on October 2). Trading Central (tradingcentral.com) is a commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (jasper.moiseiwitsch@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November 01, 2015 21:27 ET (0