\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2 's Yuan Could Rally Again on  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 , Yuan-Won Link -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 221 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2015\par 02:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/CNY has taken on a bearish technical bias with Friday's rally of the yuan - due to suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The spot USD/CNY pair is now under the daily Ichimoku Cloud support zone and inside the Bollinger downtrend channel, signalling an increased likelihood of further yuan strength versus the U.S. dollar. The yuan rallied 0.6% against the greenback Friday as agents of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold large quantities of U.S. dollars in the offshore market, says a trader. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  may persist in boosting the value of the offshore-traded yuan to coincide with that of the onshore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which is typically stronger. If the two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade in tandem, it would be easier to merge them into one unit - which would augment China's push toward making the yuan an international reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  Over the weekend, authorities announced the setting up of a direct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange between China and South Korea, which could also weigh on USD/CNY due to the reduced demand for dollars from Chinese importers. USD/CNY ended Friday at 6.3175. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Editor: MNG\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 02-11-15 0138GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020151