\par\par \b \uc2 Hong Kong Stocks Brace for Impact of China Manufacturing Data -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,344 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2015\par 02:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Hong Kong investors brace for the impact of lackluster Chinese manufacturing data Monday. Hang Seng Index futures are down 2% in morning trading after the official manufacturing purchasing managers' index released Sunday was unchanged at 49.8 in October, indicating that the sector contracted slightly that month. "The data reinforce our view that the last \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  interest rate cut was to pre-empt weak October activity data," ING says. Disappointing data could prompt global investor sentiment to become more "risk-off", and may trigger another round soon of interest rate cuts in China, the house adds. Another gauge of Chinese manufacturing, the independently conducted Caixin manufacturing PMI, will be released soon after the market opens. Analysts forecast a consensus reading of 47.6 in October versus 47.2 the month before.\par \par \uc2  Hong Kong-listed bank and railway stocks are also in focus after a slew of earnings results.(Dominique.fong@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @dominiquefong) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0108 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Japanese stocks are down following worse-than-expected China's official manufacturing purchasing managers' index, says Mitsushige Akino, chief fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management. The steel sector, sensitive to China's economic activities, is the worst performer among Topix subindexes. Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nippon Steel & Sumitomo Metal Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (5401.TO) are down 4.9% at Y2,350.0. He also says some market participants are still selling the shares because the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  didn't take additional stimulus measures on Friday. The Nikkei is down 1.5% at 18793.57.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0055 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/CAD stays below its key resistance at 1.3130 and remains under pressure. Both descending 20-period and 50-period intraday moving averages maintain a bearish bias. Meanwhile the intraday relative strength index lacks upward momentum. A first target to the downside is at 1.3035. A break below this level would open the way to further weakness towards 1.2990 in extension. Only a break above the key resistance at 1.3130 would call for further upside towards 1.3200 at first and then towards 1.3240 in extension.This content was produced exclusively for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2  by Trading Central (tradingcentral.com), an investment research and commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (kenneth.howe@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0048 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The EUR/JPY stays above its support at 132.15 and is moving sideways. Even though a continuation of the consolidation cannot be ruled out, its extent should be limited. Further upside is expected with the next horizontal resistance and overlap set at 133.55 at first. A break above this level would call for further advance towards 133.90 in extension. Only a break below the horizontal support at 132.15 would open the way to further weakness towards 131.60 at first. A second alternative target is set at 131.10 in extension.This content was produced exclusively for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2  by Trading Central (tradingcentral.com), an investment research and commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (kenneth.howe@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0044 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The EUR/GBP stays below its key resistance at 0.7200 and remains on the downside. The 20-period intraday moving average has just crossed below its 50-period one, calling for further decline. The intraday relative strength index is around 50 and lacks strong upward momentum. A first target to the downside is set at 0.7120. A break below this level would open the way to further weakness towards the horizontal support and overlap at 0.7095 in extension. Only a break above the key resistance at 0.7200 would call for further upside towards 0.7235 at first and then towards 0.7260 in extension. This content was produced exclusively for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2  by Trading Central (tradingcentral.com), an investment research and commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (kenneth.howe@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0039 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/MYR is up from its Friday close, but may struggle to sustain its rally unless it overcomes technical resistance around 4.2950. A Monday closing above that level would activate the Bollinger uptrend channel, which could encourage speculative buying of the U.S. dollar. The ringgit may come under pressure versus the greenback if Malaysia's manufacturing PMI data due at 0215 GMT disappoints. Regional economic weakness, as seen in China's contractionary manufacturing PMI released over the weekend, could also surface in Malaysia. Crude oil prices were sliding through October, likely affecting profit margins and thereby production figures of Malaysia's oil-related industries. USD/MYR is now 4.2960 from its Friday close of 4.2920. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0024 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/KRW is up slightly Monday, implying a mildly weaker won versus the U.S. dollar, ahead of South Korea's manufacturing data due at 0140 GMT. Signs of slowing growth in China - where manufacturing PMI released over the weekend showed that the factory sector remains in contraction - could be a precursor to similarly lackluster data from South Korea. USD/KRW may rally past the 20-day Bollinger mid resistance line at 1,143 if manufacturing data is poor. If that technical barrier is breached on a daily closing basis, there will be a higher probability of greater strength for the greenback in the days ahead. The benchmark \f28 \uc2 \f2 ICE\f28 \uc2 \f2  U.S. dollar index - which acts as a leading indicator for most USD/Asia pairs - is still in an uptrend despite a pullback over the last two sessions due to a recovery of the euro and the yen. USD/KRW is now 1,140.6 from its Friday close of 1,140.1. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0010 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Singapore dollar may find strength from the rally of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yuan on Friday - which was likely due to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The yuan rallied 0.6% against the greenback late Friday as agents of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold large quantities of U.S. dollars in the offshore market, says a trader. USD/SGD could drift lower toward the 1.3970 base of the daily Ichimoku Cloud support zone if traders think China wants the yuan to appreciate some more - at least for now. The deliberate move to boost the yuan could be more than a one-off, as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  has been attempting to converge the value of the offshore-traded yuan with that of the onshore \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - which is typically stronger. If the two \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can trade in tandem, it increases the simplicity of merging them into one unit - which would help with China's push toward making the yuan an international reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . USD/SGD is now 1.4002 from its Friday close of 1.4005. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0458 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] One bright spot in South Korea's dark and gloomy trade data in October was the surge in shipments of mobile handsets. Details of trade data show Korean exports of smartphones and their parts rose 42% on-year in October while nearly all other Korean export items--including cars, steel, ships, machinery and semiconductors--slumped. The trade ministry in Seoul attributes rising cellphone exports to the launch of new Galaxy and V10 models from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung\f28 \uc2 \f2  and LG. By region, exports to China fell 8% on-year in October--a fourth consecutive month of decline. Shipments to the U.S. and the E.U. also slid 11.4% and 12.5% respectively in October. Korea's total shipments in October shrank 15.8% from a year earlier--the fastest decline in six years. The latest reading marks a sharper-than-expected fall. Exports have been in decline for 10 consecutive months this year. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: CNG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November 01, 2015 20:08 ET (01:08 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020151102ebb20006m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
