\par\par \b \uc2 USD/Asia May Drift Until Manufacturing PMIs Released -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 189 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2015\par 01:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 2359 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/Asia may drift around till manufacturing PMI data from various countries, notably South Korea, Taiwan, Malaysia, and Indonesia, are released. Over the weekend, China's official manufacturing purchasing managers' index was unchanged at 49.8 in October from a month ago, missing the 50.0 median expectation. There still appears to be an air of risk aversion in Asia stocks, which may transpose itself to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, possibly due to apprehension ahead of this week's U.S. non-farm payrolls, which could significantly affect the odds of the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  raising interest rates in December. On Friday, a 0.6% jump in the yuan's value -- due to suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- could steer some correlated \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the South Korea won and the Singapore dollar toward strength. An offshore trader said that the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  was suspected of selling U.S. dollars to boost the yuan in the offshore market. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 02-11-15 0031GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020151102ebb20000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
