\par\par \b \uc2 USD/Asia May Drift Until Manufacturing PMIs Released -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,716 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2015\par 00:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 2359 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/Asia may drift around till manufacturing PMI data from various countries, notably South Korea, Taiwan, Malaysia, and Indonesia, are released. Over the weekend, China's official manufacturing purchasing managers' index was unchanged at 49.8 in October from a month ago, missing the 50.0 median expectation. There still appears to be an air of risk aversion in Asia stocks, which may transpose itself to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, possibly due to apprehension ahead of this week's U.S. non-farm payrolls, which could significantly affect the odds of the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  raising interest rates in December. On Friday, a 0.6% jump in the yuan's value -- due to suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- could steer some correlated \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the South Korea won and the Singapore dollar toward strength. An offshore trader said that the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  was suspected of selling U.S. dollars to boost the yuan in the offshore market. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2352 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Kingsgate Consolidated Ltd\uc2 . (KCN.AU) built its business on a suite of gold projects in Asia, but analysts say its future may lie elsewhere: in South America. Last week, Kingsgate announced a plan to sell its Challenger gold mine in Australia--a positive move, says Macquarie, as it relieves the miner of closure and rehabilitation costs but allows it to retain some exposure with a royalty stream. "Commentary for Kingsgate indicates that [its Nueva Esperanza gold project in Chile], and South America more broadly speaking, is where management sees the future of the company," says Macquarie. Continued exploration at Nueva Esperanza will look to build up the miner's potential resource there, notes Macquarie, which says "communication and execution of Kingsgate's South American strategy is likely to provide the most meaningful catalysts for the stock." Still, it keeps an Underperform rating and A$0.30 target on the stock, which last traded at A$0.66/share. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; Twitter: @RhiannonHoyle)\par \par \uc2 2350 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  keeps an overweight call on Mineral Resources (MIN.AU), despite the Australian iron-ore producer's 1Q shipments falling short of its forecasts. The broker had expected Mineral Resources to ship 11 million tons in FY16, but 1Q volumes of 2.6 million tons points to an annualized figure of 10.5 million tons. The shortfall could be explained by throughput restrictions at the port of Kwinana in Western Australia, which should be temporary, it says. "Although we reduce the target price (by 24% to A$6.80/share), the balance sheet and free cash flow recovery post FY16 keep us positive on the equity," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says. MIN last traded at A$4.30. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2348 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Medical imaging and diagnostics company Primary Health Care (PRY.AU) is about to set investors' pulses racing, says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . The broker is tipping Primary's share price to rise over the next two months, and ascribes a more than 80% chance of this scenario happening. "This is because the stock has traded off recently, making short-term valuation much more compelling," analyst Sean Laaman says. PRY last traded at A$3.71. "Primary shares have underperformed due to the company's change in remuneration structure for its new doctors and the associated EPS uncertainty this brings," Laaman says. "Our analysis suggests that the shares have overreacted and that a buying opportunity has been presented." Primary is due to hold its annual meeting of shareholders on Nov. 26, and \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  thinks management will provide FY16 guidance for the first time. That may dissolve the market's concerns by providing some certainty on the outlook, it says. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2340 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/JPY may be weighed on by China's slowdown concerns. Data showed over the weekend China's official manufacturing purchasing managers' index was unchanged at 49.8 in October from a month ago, undershooting the 50.0 level expected by economists. Signs of deterioration in China's economy tend to encourage buying of JPY against AUD and other higher yielding, riskier \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The USD/JPY is at 120.53, compared to 120.62 late Friday in New York. The AUD/JPY is at 85.94, down from 86.07. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2337 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 BHP Billiton Ltd\uc2 . (BHP.AU) is spending less to ship iron ore from its Australian mines, a positive shift at a time of cooling prices. Macquarie says that was a key message from a recent investor roundtable with the miner. "Through optimizing its shipping fleet mix and increasing the amount of direct load from mine to ship, BHP believes it has already achieved an increase in shipping capacity of 8 million tons per annum by increasing direct to ship ore and reducing the ship turnaround time, which has fallen 18% over the past two years," it says in a note. "BHP also claims its iron-ore freight costs are 20% lower than its Australian-based peers," says Macquarie. BHP trades down 0.6% at A$22.89/share. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; Twitter: @RhiannonHoyle)\par \par \uc2 2332 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Japanese stocks may be weighed on by concerns about the slowdown of the Chinese economy. Data showed on Sunday China's official manufacturing purchasing managers' index was unchanged at 49.8 in October from a month ago, below the 50.0 forecast by economists. Nikkei 225 December futures ended Chicago trading at 18880 on Friday, compared to an earlier close in Osaka at 19090. The Nikkei ended up 0.8% at 19083.10. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2329 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Westpac\uc2 's (WBC.AU) annual result contains few surprises, after many of the key figures were pre-released last month, says Macquarie Research. The broker notes impairment charges edged up to 13 basis points for the second half of fiscal 2015 from 11 basis points a year earlier, driven primarily by higher write-offs in the Lloyds portfolio and growth in the retail and business banking unsecured portfolio, partially offset by continued improvements in asset quality and reducing customer delinquencies. However the broker notes the balance sheet appears in good shape following its recent capital raising, with its common equity Tier 1 capital ratio increasing 74 basis points from 8.76% to 9.50%. Macquarie keeps an Outperform rating on the stock. (rebecca.thurlow@wsj.com; @beckthurlow)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Activity in Australia's manufacturing sector has recorded its fourth month of expansion in a row, with exports supporting the result. The Australian PMI fell by 1.9 points in October to 50.2, with readings above 50 indicating expansion in activity. The result marked the longest run of expansionary readings since July 2010, according to the \uc2 Australian Industry Group\uc2 . AIG chief executive, Innes Willox said exports sales were behind the outcome. "A strong export performance in October helped manufacturing hold onto the gains made by the sector over the previous three months, clocking up the longest expansion in five years. Still, the process of broadening the base of growth across the economy remains gradual and tentative, he added. "There is clearly scope for stimulatory measures to boost domestic activity," he added. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2325 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Expect volatility in the Australian dollar this week, says Joe Capurso, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at CBA. The U.S. October ISM and non-farm payrolls data should undermine the Australian dollar as will another soft Caixin China PMI, he says. But the largest potential for volatility in the Aussie is closer to home, he adds. Local economists are deeply divided about whether the RBA will cut the cash rate or not on Tuesday. The OIS market is pricing a 42% chance of a rate cut by the RBA on Tuesday. Therefore, there will be a material reaction in the Australian dollar after the RBA announces its decision and possibly further market reactions on Thursday with RBA Governor \uc2 Glenn Stevens\uc2 ' speaking and on Friday as the RBA announces fresh economic forecasts, he says. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 UBS\uc2  lowers its price target on household fittings supplier \uc2 GWA Group\uc2  (GWA.AU) by 6.1% to A$2.46/share because the falling Australian dollar is starting to eat into its earnings. The Aussie fell around 9% against the greenback in the first-quarter of FY16, adding to headwinds for GWA that include its underperforming Gainsborough door \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 UBS\uc2  notes that GWA has raised prices for some of its products, but these will take time to become fully effective. At the same time, the Aussie could well fall further. Positives for GWA include the potential for additional capital management and improved underlying cash generation, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. "However, this is balanced by a consistent wide disparity between 'underlying' versus statutory earnings and risks in terms of the Gainsborough turnaround, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 , market share losses and achieving cost outs," analyst Doug Macphillamy says. \uc2 UBS\uc2  stays at neutral. GWA last traded at A$2.48. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2308 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Shares of Australia's largest banks may be a little more expensive than shares of the largest U.S. banks relative to their forward earnings, but on average the Australians offer almost three times more forward yield, Deutsche Bank analysts Andrew Triggs and Anthony Hoo say. They think \uc2 Australia & New Zealand Banking Group\uc2  (ANZ.AU) and \uc2 Westpac Banking Corp\uc2 . (WBC.AU)--trading at 14% and 11% respectively below their average price-earnings ratio over the past five years--offer the best value. ANZ also promises the highest 12-month forward dividend yield at 6.3%, compared with a 12-month forward dividend yield of 6.0% among Australia's largest lenders and 2.1% among large-cap U.S. banks. ANZ was trading at A$27.21 before markets opened Monday, while \uc2 Westpac\uc2  was at A$31.38. (vera.sprothen@wsj.com; Twitter: @verasydney)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 01, 2015 18:59 ET (23:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020151101ebb1000b2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
