\par\par \uc2 Asiastox\par \b \uc2 Asian Shares on Track for Best Month in Six Years\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chao Deng  \par \uc2 855 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2015\par 02:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top Global Market Stories\par \uc2 DJTGMS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Asian markets are headed for their best month in more than six years on Friday, fueled by hopes that central banks around the world will inject stimulus into their economies.\par \par \uc2 But investors have grown weary over the past week and momentum has flagged.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's decision Friday to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/bank-of-japan-keeps-monetary-policy-unchanged-1446176117"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 keep its monetary policy unchanged  }}}\uc2  demonstrates why: Hopes for more easing could easily disappoint if policy makers don't deliver.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrained from introducing further easing, keeping its annual asset purchase target at \u165\'00\'A5 80 trillion, despite building expectations of stimulus amid disappointing economic data.\par \par \uc2 Markets in the region took the decision in stride. Japan's Nikkei Stock Average rose 0.8%, to 19083.10, its highest closing level in two-months.\par \par \uc2 While shares in China and Australia also gained after the decision, the Shanghai Composite Index finished off 0.1% and Australia's S&P \uc2 ASX\uc2  fell 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's Kospi fell 0.2% and Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index lost 0.8%.\par \par \uc2 "The general trend is an ongoing recovery from the August and September selloff," said Angus Nicholson, market analyst at brokerage IG. The big pressure point on markets then\u8212\'20\'14worries about a collapse in China\u8212\'20\'14is less of a factor, giving the region's market momentum, he says.\par \par \uc2 Despite the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's decision Friday, economists aren't wiping prospects for more easing off the table. Credit Suisse economist Takashi Shiono said he believes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably will wait until next spring, when it has a clearer picture of inflation that isn't distorted by weak oil prices.\par \par \uc2 Stimulus hopes have helped put the MSCI Asia Pacific Index on track for its best month since May 2009, up 8% month-to-date in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  terms through Thursday. It is headed for its first month of gains after four straight months of losses.\par \par \uc2 China's markets are leading the region's rebound, with the Shanghai Composite Index gaining 11% after four-straight negative months. The Hang Seng Index gained 8.6% for the month, after five-straight months of losses.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei Stock Average closed out the month with gains of 10%, its best monthly performance since April 2013.\par \par \uc2 October's momentum is a turnaround from the summer months, when fears about China's slowdown rocked global markets. Despite the sharp rebound, investors wary of policy makers' limitations have said markets already have priced in the potential benefits of easing.\par \par \uc2 Ilya Feygin, managing director at New York-based brokerage WallachBeth Capital, said he sees central banks as the driver of fourth-quarter performance but that he is watching Shanghai as a harbinger of what is to come for the region.\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite fell 0.9% this week, snapping three weeks of gains, even after the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  cut interest rates last Friday for the sixth time since November. The Hang Seng Index is down 2.2% this week, after rallying for four straight weeks.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday at the conclusion of its annual meeting, China's Communist Party approved a five-year plan that vows, among other goals, to encourage consumption.\par \par \uc2 After the close of Asian markets Thursday, Chinese authorities also announced they would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/china-abandons-one-child-policy-1446116462"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 abandon the country's one-child policy  }}}\uc2 , instead allowing all Chinese couples to have two children. While officials didn't provide a time frame, stocks are already moving in anticipation of a baby boomlet.\par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong, stroller and car-seat maker \uc2 Goodbaby International Holdings Ltd\uc2 . rose 2.3% and infant-formula maker Biostimes Interntional Holdings Ltd. gained 5.1%. Shenzhen-listed Beingmate Baby & Child Food Co. rose by the 10% daily maximum set by Chinese regulators.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, the yen traded as strong as \u165\'00\'A5 120.40 to one U.S. dollar following the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's announcement. It has since weakened back to \u165\'00\'A5 120.99, roughly flat compared with its level late Thursday in Asia.\par \par \uc2 The onshore Chinese yuan suddenly rose to its strongest in three weeks Friday afternoon, to 6.3200 per U.S. dollar. It was last at 6.3205, marking a 0.6% gain compared with its close Thursday, the biggest daily percentage gain since March 2014.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push up the offshore yuan on Thursday, to close its gap against its onshore counterpart, ahead of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's decision in November on whether to include the yuan to its basket of global reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the gap between the offshore and onshore yuan had been at its widest since August, when the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  devalued the yuan.\par \par \uc2 By Friday, analysts said investors rushed to cover their shorts on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  offshore, driving it up 0.5% late in the day.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, brent crude oil was down 0.4% at $48.60 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Prices for gold were flat at $1,147.40 a troy ounce.\par \par \uc2 Takashi Nakamichi, Kosaku Narioka and Fiona Law contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Chao Deng at \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTGMS1120151030ebau0000y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
