\par\par \uc2 China News\par \b \uc2 China's Leaders to Approve Economic Blueprint\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jeremy Page  and Mark Magnier   \par \uc2 894 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2015\par 01:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top Global Market Stories\par \uc2 DJTGMS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING\u8212\'20\'14China's Communist Party this week is expected to approve an economic blueprint for scaling back the role of the state over the next five years, while offering clues about the leadership's appetite for bold change {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/china-economic-growth-falls-below-7-for-first-time-since-2009-1445221368"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in the face of slowing growth }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The four-day meeting of the Central Committee\u8212\'20\'14the party's top 300 or so leaders\u8212\'20\'14is a test of whether the political clout President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  has amassed since coming to power three years ago has enabled him to overcome resistance to the reform program he unveiled in 2013.\u160\'00\'A0\par \par \uc2 As China's leaders struggle to steer the economy onto a different growth path, a central question around the meeting, which begins Monday, will be whether much-used reform mantras, some of which date back to a similar plenum in 1993, will translate into decisive action.\par \par \uc2 "The next five years will be very important for restructuring the economy," said Jia Qingguo, associate dean at \uc2 Peking University\uc2 . "The traditional way of managing the economy is meeting more and more obstacles."\par \par \uc2 Some party insiders say President Xi has neglected the economy in favor of pursuing his domestic political agenda, especially his anticorruption campaign. But the president's supporters say he still faces fierce resistance to reforms from powerful business and political figures, including some retired leaders, with interests in maintaining the status quo. Personnel changes at the meeting will likely indicate whether Mr. Xi is able to promote key allies ahead of the next big leadership shuffle in 2017.\par \par \uc2 The meeting, known as a plenum, could also be an indicator of whether the Chinese leadership, shaken by a stock-market crash over the summer, is shying away from bolder reforms and focusing instead on stemming a steeper-than-expected economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 Once largely ignored by outsiders, the labyrinthine workings of the Communist Party have taken on more meaning for the global economy, in which China accounts for a third of growth. Markets tumbled this summer because of concerns over \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slowdown, as well as Beijing's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the stock market and its surprise \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation.\par \par \uc2 The meeting starts a week after China posted third-quarter growth of 6.9% and as officials are signaling that the full-year growth target of about 7% may not be met.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/chinas-central-bank-cuts-rates-1445601495"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 China cut interest rates for the sixth time in less than a year }}}\uc2  and reduced banks' reserve requirements, the latest in a series of measures to put a floor under a slowdown that has proved more persistent than leaders had expected.\par \par \uc2 In a speech Friday at the Central Party School, Premier \uc2 Li Keqiang\uc2  emphasized that the 7% growth benchmark is approximate, according to official reports. "We never said we must defend any target to the death, but that the economy should operate within a reasonable range," Mr. Li said, according to the central government's website.\par \par \uc2 Economists have called on Beijing to set a five-year growth benchmark of 6.5% or less so it can focus on structural reform rather than scrambling to meet a too-high target.\par \par \uc2 However, the party has pledged to double the size of the economy and personal income in the decade leading up to 2021, its 100th anniversary. That would require growth of close to 7%; trying to get there would likely worsen China's debt and overcapacity problems.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, a top \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said China should be able to maintain growth in the 6% to 7% range over the next three to five years.\par \par \uc2 The closed-door meeting, held in a heavily guarded hotel in Beijing, will likely be followed by an official account of the proceedings, but details about the five-year plan aren't likely to be released until March, when China's rubber-stamp parliament meets.\par \par \uc2 Even as Beijing says it wants to reduce the state's clout, some experts say plenums and five-year plans still have their place by giving government-owned banks, companies and bureaucrats a blueprint to move ahead.\par \par \uc2 "The five-year plans provide a useful organizing framework for the government's reform agenda, despite the plans' rather paternalistic approach," said \uc2 Cornell University\uc2  professor Eswar Prasad, a former China head for the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While China is aiming to shift toward an economy driven by consumption, a big focus of the plan is likely to be megaprojects such as the One Belt, One Road plan, one of the most ambitious infrastructure projects in history.\u160\'00\'A0\par \par \uc2 Its aim is to better connect the Chinese economy with the rest of Asia, Africa, the Middle East and Europe, but its form is more reminiscent of old-style investment-led growth than reform.\par \par \uc2 The president has made it clear he is pressing on with the anticorruption campaign: Last week, the agency in charge of it announced that it would expand inspections to major state firms, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , financial regulators and China's two stock exchanges.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Xi could also enhance his control of the military at the plenum if he succeeds in engineering the promotion of Gen. \uc2 Liu Yuan\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14a personal friend who is the son of former Chinese President Liu Shaoqi\u8212\'20\'14to the Central Military Commission, which controls the armed forces.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jeremy Page at  and Mark Magnier at \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTGMS1120151026ebaq00008\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
