\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China says outflows normal, no panic capital flight\b0\par\par\uc2 402 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2015\par 08:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, quotes)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Oct 22 (Reuters) - Recent outflows of money from China are "normal" and not a sign of panic capital flight, a senior official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator said on Thursday, downplaying fears over growing outflows as the economy slows.\par \par \uc2 China is confident in keeping international payments and receipts balanced in the future thanks to a positive economic outlook, Wang Xiaoyi, deputy head of the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE) told a news conference.\par \par \uc2 "Current changes (in capital flows) are normal, which should not be regarded as capital flight," Wang said.\par \par \uc2 The main reasons for recent outflows were a greater willingness by companies and individuals to hold \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and moves by firms which were adjusting their foreign debt structures and increasing investment abroad, Wang said.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves posted their biggest quarterly decline on record in July-September, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilise the yuan and calm sentiment after it unexpectedly devalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Aug. 11, shocking global markets.\par \par \uc2 China's commercial banks sold a net 729.6 billion yuan of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of clients in September, cooling from August's 807 billion yuan but still showing signs of capital outflows, data released by SAFE earlier on Thursday showed.\par \par \uc2 Some investors had already been withdrawing funds from China and other emerging markets well before its devaluation on concerns about the extent of China's economic slowdown and possible interest rate rises by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The recent decline in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves was within a controllable range and government activity in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the third quarter was normal conduct, rather than large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Wang said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to keep the yuan steady after it devalued the yuan by nearly 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 Wang said reforms in the exchange rate system would have positive effects on stabilising cross-border capital flows in the future, and said no direct connection can be seen between the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reforms and capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 The SAFE said it would step up monitoring of cross-border capital flows while studying measures including \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transaction fees, tobin tax to curb short-term, large cross- boarder capital flows. (Reporting By Xiaoyi Shao and Nicholas Heath; Editing by Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-10-22T07:00:06.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020151022ebam005uu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
