\par\par \b \uc2 CHINA MONEY-Beijing's forceful moves prompt traders to change weak yuan views\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lu Jianxin and Pete Sweeney   \par \uc2 701 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2015\par 07:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Oct 21 (Reuters) - Sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and policy changes are prompting traders to change their views and raise year-end targets for the yuan, after its surprise devaluation in August sparked fears the Beijing wanted a sharp fall.\par \par \uc2 The moves by the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 were prompted by market panic after the devaluation. The yuan dropped around 3 percent against the dollar in a few days.\par \par \uc2 That rattled global investors about Beijing's commitment to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market reform and made some exit yuan assets. Issuance in offshore yuan markets dried up as traders priced the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a sharp discount.\par \par \uc2 Just before the devaluation, the yuan was at 6.2097 to the dollar. Right afterwards, many traders saw it softening to 6.5 by year-end.\par \par \uc2 However, while the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in onshore and offshore markets eventually stabilised the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders and economists said such action were ultimately unsustainable, as China would rapidly run down its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 Fortunately for Beijing, traders now feel differently about the yuan, in part because of steps to cap capital outflows. And this month's launch of the China International Payment System (CIPs), to facilitate trade settlement, is expected to boost offshore yuan usage.\par \par \uc2 "The yuan is now performing more in line with other emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Li Liuyang, an analyst at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 "If the (U.S.) \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  doesn't raise interest rates by the end of this year, the yuan may rise to as much as 6.3 per dollar; otherwise it can weaken to as much as 6.45."\par \par \uc2 Li's estimates were echoed by traders surveyed by Reuters. On Wednesday morning, the yuan was at about 6.343 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 A KINDER \uc2 U.S. TREASURY\par \par \uc2 Data also points to a gradually stabilising yuan.\par \par \uc2 The decline of the yuan's real effective exchange rate (REER), or its value against the trade-weighted basket after adjustments based on inflation, eased to 0.32 percent in September, from 0.61 percent in August, according to the \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2  (BIS).\par \par \uc2 Even the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 , which has consistently singled out the undervalued yuan and argued for its appreciation, was kinder in its new semi-annual report on trading partners' \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. It said the yuan needs to appreciate more but was below its "appropriate medium-term valuation".\par \par \uc2 Domestic traders naturally hope the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  will approve the yuan's inclusion in its Special Drawing Rights basket in discussions expected next month.\par \par \uc2 Being in the SDR basket "can stimulate more demand for the yuan and thus boost its value," said Liu Dongliang, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at China Merchant Bank in Shenzhen.\par \par \uc2 HSBC\uc2  said on Tuesday that China's yuan "ticks all the right boxes" for inclusion in the SDR basket.\par \par \uc2 China this month told the \uc2 IMF\uc2  it had subscribed to its Special Data Dissemination Standard (SDDS). Data shows the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  already began publishing its data in line with the standard in July.\par \par \uc2 LONGER TRADING HOURS?\par \par \uc2 And China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will soon extend yuan trading hours to 2330 local time (1530 GMT) to cover Europe's market day, sources say.\par \par \uc2 For all the steps taken, regulators remain on guard against bearish sentiment that might cause capital flight.\par \par \uc2 Beyond apparently instructing state banks to buy yuan and sell dollars, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  also tightened rules on trading of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives, increasing corporate trading costs to cool speculation of yuan depreciation.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has asked banks to strengthen supervision of foreign-held non-resident accounts (NRA) purchases of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in China. (For a FACTBOX, please click on )\par \par \uc2 And the weakening economy is a cause for concern.\par \par \uc2 "The authorities can and will continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the yuan from dropping too much," said a trader at an Asian bank in Hong Kong. "But they will not be able to sustain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strengthening in a weak economy." (Reporting by the Shanghai Newsroom and Lu Jianxin; Editing by Richard Borsuk) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-10-21T06:18:23.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020151021ebal005ni\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
