\par\par \b \uc2 Nike\uc2  Continues to Escape the DJIA Curse -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,304 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2015\par 20:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 14:55 EDT - Already easily the best-performing Dow component of 2015, \uc2 Nike\uc2  (NKE) is atop today's chart thanks to an upgrade to buy at \uc2 BB&T\uc2 , which had been apprehensive about the stock's valuation. "We underestimated, however, the speed with which NKE was able to reposition the Chinese market," where it's logging double-digit order growth. The bank is also reacting to the sportswear giant's recent analyst gathering, where guidance "represents an acceleration in sales and EPS growth." With efforts such as expanding the Jordan and Converse brands and the looming Summer Olympics, \uc2 BB&T\uc2  is "more constructive on the near-term opportunity for continued momentum." It boosts FY EPS views and sets a $148 price target. NKE rises 2.2% to $133.33 in hitting fresh record highs; it's up 39% for 2015. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 14:48 EDT - \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 , unlike other financial services firms such as \uc2 Visa Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 MasterCard Inc\uc2 ., does not run a lab devoted to innovation. Instead, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  prefers to promote innovation throughout its technology and \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, using annual meetings with startups, teams dedicated to assessing emerging technologies and a push to patent inventions, says \uc2 Cathy Bessant\uc2 , chief \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and technology officer. The bank runs an annual technology summit for startups to showcase new products, and usually adopts 16% to 17% of the products presented, according to Ms. Bessant. (kim.nash@wsj.com; @knash99)\par \par \uc2 14:47 EDT - A federal appeals court upholds key components of two gun laws passed shortly after the December 2012 massacre in Newtown, Conn., a victory for gun-control backers in New York and Connecticut. The Second Circuit ruled parts of laws passed in both states, which expanded the definition of prohibited automatic and semiautomatic weapons don't violate the 2nd Amendment. The laws, which represent some of the strictest in the country, were challenged as unconstitutional by groups including gun owners and firearms dealers. (ashby.jones@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:43 EDT - \uc2 Aetna\uc2  (AET) says it won the shareholder vote it needed to issues stock to \uc2 Humana\uc2  (HUM) stockholders and complete its acquisition of the Medicare-focused insurer. The approval, which had been expected, removes one hurdle to the deal; HUM owners are expected to vote later this afternoon. But the acquisition still faces challenges, including Justice Department antitrust reviewers and a gauntlet of state regulators. AET says of the 287.7M shares voted on at today's special meeting, 99% favored the deal. (anna.mathews@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:41 EDT - Weight Watchers (WTW) got an investment from an unusual source -- as Oprah Winfrey took a 10% stake in the struggling company, while getting a board seat. The type of deal is commonly referred to as a pipe, or Private Investment in Public Equity--and is sometimes used as a fallback option when a struggling company strikes out on a full sale. In 2014, Crocs took a $200M investment from \uc2 Blackstone\uc2  after it abandoned an effort to sell itself. \uc2 Avon Products\uc2  (AVP) is also running a process for private-equity firms to take a pipe, WSJ has reported. In most instances, the investments come from private-equity firms looking to put capital to use and influence decision making. WTW surges 120% to $14.95. (dana.mattioli@wsj.com; @DanaMattioli)\par \par \uc2 14:41 EDT - From the cause-and-effect department comes an \uc2 S&P\uc2  report which finds millennials' driving habits are screwing up road funding. They drive less and are in more-fuel-efficient cars than generations before, squeezing revenue from the federal gas tax that's the main source for funding highway-improvement projects. "This drop in funds available to construct and repair the country's infrastructure could...weigh on growth prospects for US GDP as well as states' economies." \uc2 S&P\uc2  adds "where states and municipalities choose to replace the lost federal funds with locally derived revenues," credit quality could be hurt. The federal gas tax is 18.4c/gallon, a level which hasn't changed since 1993. The amount raises $25B/year. If indexed to inflation, the tax would be 30c and generating $42B. (jeff.bennett@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:35 EDT - The biggest fund at junk-bond-focused \uc2 Phoenix Investment Adviser\uc2  declined 24% through the end of August, according to investor documents. Still, founder \uc2 Jeffrey Peskind\uc2  views the sustained pullback for distressed assets as an "unbelievable potential buying opportunity" given the overall strength of the US economy. (rob.copeland@wsj.com; @realrobcopeland)\par \par \uc2 14:33 EDT - Hedge-fund and private-equity managers began piling into debt issued by troubled energy companies the past year, but the trade is causing pain. An energy fund at \uc2 Magnetar Capital\uc2  is down 12% while Brigade Capital's main hedge fund is having its worst stretch since 2008. "A lot of hot money chased into what we believe are insolvent companies at best," says \uc2 Paul Twitchell\uc2 , partner at hedge fund \uc2 Whitebox Advisors\uc2 . (rob.copeland@wsj.com; @realrobcopeland)\par \par \uc2 14:31 EDT - Mexican stock-market heavyweight \uc2 America Movil\uc2  (\uc2 AMX\uc2 , AMX.MX) is gaining ahead of the company's after-the-close 3Q report. Results are seen being little changed from a year earlier, according to the median estimate of equities analysts polled by WSJ. Shares rise 1.5% in Mexico City, cutting the year's decline to 12%. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:29 EDT - Turbocharger maker \uc2 BorgWarner\uc2  was downgraded to underweight by \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  due to "long-term" secular challenges including lower fuel prices and potentially slower growth from its biggest customer -- \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 . "We believe the stock deserves to be cheaper given the moving pieces in the fundamental environment impacting the earnings outlook," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  said. The big challenge is the fallout from the VW emissions cheating scandal. Slowing car sales could hurt \uc2 BorgWarner\uc2  since the company makes 17% of its revenue from VW alone. Shares fall 2.6% to $42.48, down 18% over the last three months. (jeff.bennett@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:27 EDT - Great Lakes (GLDD) hits a 6-year low today in the wake of the dredging-services firm pulling 2015 guidance amid "unanticipated project losses" and saying its chairman has left the board. But longtime bull Imperial Capital remains resolute, contending GLDD "is well-positioned to benefit from a multiyear uplift in marine-construction activity given its strong dredging capabilities." But the firm admits prospects for GLDD's environmental and remediation business "are more uncertain." DA Davidson, which cuts its price target a buck to $6, still sees "near-term growth in the dredging business and expect these \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  would attract substantial interest, particularly if separated from the losses in the environmental & remediation segment." But investors look to be throwing in the towel with GLDD down 23% at $4.11. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 14:18 EDT - Foreign central banks sold a net $41.1B of Treasurys in August, smashing the old monthly record of $29.7B set in August 2007, says RBC. The group has dumped $121B the latest 6 months, extending a record pace of sales. The August reading "to some extent confirms suspicions there was large \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling in August to fund \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the wake of the \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation," says RBC. But it also notes while the magnitude of sales have been large by historical standards, when adjusting for the level of marketable debt outstanding the swing is not unprecedented. The $121B makes up roughly 1% of marketable debt, and foreign-official sales have been about that large in 1991, 1998 and 2000, says RBC. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 19, 2015 14:55 ET (18:55 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020151019ebaj003fh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
