\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-China  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves post record quarterly fall as cenbank steps up yuan support\b0\par\par\uc2 517 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2015\par 08:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quarterly data milestone, fresh analyst comment)               \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Oct 7 (Reuters) - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves posted their biggest quarterly decline on record in July-September, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilise the yuan and calm sentiment after an unexpected devaluation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had jolted global markets.               \par \par \uc2 China's reserves, the world's largest, dropped $43.3 billion to $3.514 trillion last month, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Wednesday, and were down by about $180 billion in the third quarter in their largest ever quarterly fall, according to Reuters data going back to 1980.               \par \par \uc2 The devaluation of the yuan on Aug. 11, and the consequent fall in reserves have raised questions about how sustainable China's efforts to support the yuan are, as capital trickles out of the country due to fears of a deepening economic slowdown and prospects of rising U.S. interest rates.               \par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the reserves to fall further.               \par \par \uc2 "The decline in China's foreign reserves, while less than market expected, still shows that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued the market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past month," said Singapore-based Zhou Hao, senior economist in Asia at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 "As PBoC also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the forward market in the past month, the foreign reserves will likely plunge again when these forward contracts mature," he said.               \par \par \uc2 Policy makers have been determined to calm sentiment after a summer rout in stocks, the yuan devaluation and a series of clumsy attempts by authorities to stabilise equities spread turmoil in global financial markets.               \par \par \uc2 Beijing is also pressing on with attempts to ease concerns about a cooling economy, which is growing at its slowest pace in decades.               \par \par \uc2 The yuan devaluation had sparked worries of a global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war and raised doubts about Beijing's ability to manage an economy transitioning from an investment- and exports-led model to one driven by domestic demand.               \par \par \uc2 Indeed, policy insiders have told Reuters that China was so surprised by the reaction to the devaluation that it is likely to keep the yuan on a tight leash in the near-term.                              \par \par \uc2 BEARISH YUAN?               \par \par \uc2 Although the Peoples' Bank of China has said it sees no need for further falls in the yuan, markets expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to decline more over the coming year to reflect a faltering economy.               \par \par \uc2 In the short term, however, analysts say Beijing is keen to  restore investor confidence even as a rapid volley of economic and market support measures has produced mixed results so far.               \par \par \uc2 Tim Condon, Singapore-based head of research for Asia at \uc2 ING Bank\uc2 , believes Beijing is pushing for the onshore and offshore yuan forward curves to converge again, a situation that prevailed just before the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was devalued.               \par \par \uc2 "I think their strategy to bring that about is to stablilize the spot rate, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the offshore and the onshore spot markets and hope that the economic data kind of portrays a recovering economy and confidence comes back a bit more," he said.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-10-7T07:20:55.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020151007eba7005v8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
