\par\par \b \uc2 Central Bankers Cope With A Bipolar Global Economy--WSJ's Hilsenrath\b0\par\par\uc2 625 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2015\par 07:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Korean (English)\par \uc2 RTNWK\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 <FONT FACE="Arial" class="NormalMinus1">\par \par \uc2 *DJ Central Bankers Cope With A Bipolar Global Economy--WSJ's Hilsenrath\par \par \uc2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 02, 2015 05:59 ET (09:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 *DJ World Central Banks Caught Between Two Powerful, Opposing Forces--WSJ's Hilsenrath\par \par \uc2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 02, 2015 05:59 ET (09:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 *DJ Central Banks Caught Between U.S. Possibly Raising Rates, China Weighing New Stimulus--WSJ's Hilsenrath\par \par \uc2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 02, 2015 05:59 ET (09:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 DJ Central Bankers Cope With A Bipolar Global Economy--WSJ's Hilsenrath\par \par \uc2 By Jon Hilsenrath\par \par \uc2 The fourth quarter could be a turbulent period for world central banks caught between two powerful and opposing forces--\uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  officials say they are finally nearing their first interest-rate increase in a decade as the nation's economy strengthens, while the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  considers new ways to stimulate flagging growth in the world's second-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 Some central banks--including those in Mexico, South Africa and Turkey--are warily watching the Fed for signs that they need to raise rates too or risk allowing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall to uncomfortably to low levels. Other central banks - in South Korea, Australia and New Zealand among many--are weighing whether to reduce rates or take other steps to ease financial conditions to counteract the forces of slow global growth and downward pressure on commodities prices emanating from China. Big developed-economy central banks in Europe and Japan could take new easing measures, too.\par \par \uc2 Who moves up and who moves down is a function of economic fundamentals--and exposure to the world's two dominant central banks.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's economy is heavily influenced by its big northern neighbor, so it looks likely to raise rates soon after the Fed moves. South Korea is near China is inclined to do the opposite. Turkey's economy could be damaged by sharp declines in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while New Zealand would welcome a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Fed\uc2  has been warning all year that it expected to raise rates in 2015. Fed Chairwoman \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  repeated the message in late September, a week after Fed officials decided the global economic outlook was too uncertain to move at their September policy meeting. A U.S. employment report to be released Friday, Oct. 2, could play an important role in swaying Fed officials on whether to move this month or wait until December.\par \par \uc2 Many central banks in the fourth quarter will take their cues from the Fed's next move. If the U.S. holds off on moving rates higher as it did in September, \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials might decide they need to take their own steps to ease policy. A Fed in a standstill puts upward pressure on the euro, which hurts European growth and holds down inflation. A Fed rate increase, on the other hand, would push the euro down, which could help stimulate European growth and inflation.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. and China, meantime, are influencing each other's orbits. A Fed decision to raise rates could put downward pressure on the yuan, forcing \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  further to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling. Weaker growth in China, on the other hand, could put renewed downward pressure on global commodities prices and add to squeamishness among Fed officials to act.\par \par \uc2 In a bipolar world, investors can be excused for some days of manic-depressive confusion.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jon Hilsenrath at jon.hilsenrath@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 02, 2015 05:59 ET (09:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 06-10-15 0507GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNWK00020151006eba60000i\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (china near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw)and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Datum\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/1995 bis 21/08/2023\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\t